(window.MAP_DATA = window.MAP_DATA || {})["reading_9"] = {"grade":9,"subject":"reading","units":[{"id":"R7","n":7,"title":"RIT 7","goal":"RIT","band":"211–220","skills":["Determines details that reveal characters’ thoughts","Explains character motivation","Determines main/central idea in informational text","Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots"],"vocab":[],"sets":[{"p":0,"goal":"LT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"What does Yasir’s “flat voice he had perfected” suggest about him?","o":["He is practising to become a professional speaker.","He has learned to hide his disappointment behind indifference.","He does not really care whether his father comes home.","He has a medical problem that affects the way he speaks to people."],"k":"B","sk":"Determines details that reveal characters’ thoughts","b":"211–220","g":"LT"}],"set":"1"},{"p":1,"goal":"LT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Why does Murad want to cross out the entry?","o":["He is embarrassed by the guest’s untidy handwriting.","He feels the criticism is unfair to his hardworking parents.","He thinks the angry guest has written in the wrong book by mistake.","He believes the guest was writing about another hotel."],"k":"B","sk":"Explains character motivation","b":"211–220","g":"LT"}],"set":"2"},{"p":2,"goal":"IT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"What is the central idea of the text?","o":["Diriyah was captured in 1818 after a long siege and was never rebuilt or visited again by anyone after that.","Founding Day is a national holiday that is celebrated every year in February.","Diriyah’s At-Turaif is a modest-looking but historically central site that is now being carefully restored.","Najdi architecture is more beautiful than the glass towers of modern Riyadh."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines main/central idea in informational text","b":"211–220","g":"IT"}],"set":"3"},{"p":4,"goal":"VO","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"The root **path** means “feeling.” A person who shows **apathy** toward a problem —","o":["tries hard to solve it","feels deeply about it","does not care about it","understands it fully"],"k":"C","sk":"Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots","b":"211–220","g":"VO"}],"set":"4"}]},{"id":"R8","n":8,"title":"RIT 8","goal":"RIT","band":"221–230","skills":["Analyzes dialogue to understand characters","Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots","Determines a shared idea in multiple texts","Analyzes how a character responds to complex situations"],"vocab":[],"sets":[{"p":0,"goal":"LT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Why does Yasir avoid his father after the heart attack?","o":["He is caught between long-held anger and new sympathy for a weakened man.","He is afraid that his father will ask him to study architecture.","He wants to punish his father for missing the prize-giving ceremony last year.","He has been told by the doctor not to disturb his father."],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes how a character responds to complex situations","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"What is the significance of the scale model?","o":["It proves that his father prefers small houses to large towers.","It shows that his father is planning to build a new and much larger house for the family.","It reveals that his father values the family years he missed and regrets his absence.","It explains why his father became interested in architecture as a boy."],"k":"C","sk":"Interprets symbolism in literary text","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"What contrast does the father draw in the line “I build towers for strangers. I made this one for me”?","o":["between his own building designs and those of other famous architects","between the cost of towers and the cost of houses","between the cities of Riyadh and Unaizah","between his public work and his private, personal memories"],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes dialogue to understand characters","b":"221–230","g":"LT"}],"set":"1"},{"p":1,"goal":"LT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"What does the mother’s reply “Some of it” reveal about her?","o":["She agrees completely with every single thing the angry guest wrote in the book.","She wants Murad to apologise to the guest.","She is too tired to argue with her son about it.","She can separate the unfair parts of criticism from the useful parts."],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes dialogue to understand characters","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"What does the mother mean by “A guest who is angry tells you what a guest who is polite will not”?","o":["Harsh feedback can reveal problems that polite people hide.","Polite guests are more honest than angry ones.","Angry guests should never be allowed to stay at the hotel again.","Guests should always be told what to write."],"k":"A","sk":"Determines the meaning of a nonliteral phrase","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"Which theme does the story develop?","o":["Children should not read guest books aloud.","Guesthouses high in the mountains are always cold in the winter.","Criticism, even when unpleasant, can be a source of growth.","Polite guests are the most valuable customers."],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes the development of theme in literary text","b":"221–230","g":"LT"}],"set":"2"},{"p":2,"goal":"IT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"How does the first paragraph introduce the central idea?","o":["by contrasting the site’s modest appearance with its great historical importance","by describing in great detail the long and bitter siege that destroyed the town in 1818","by explaining why the capital moved to Riyadh","by listing the materials used to build the palaces"],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes the purpose of an introduction","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"What does the detail about thick mud-brick walls show about the builders?","o":["They built quickly because they feared an attack.","They lacked the materials to build with stone or glass.","They wanted their buildings to look modest to visitors.","They adapted their buildings intelligently to the climate."],"k":"D","sk":"Makes inferences from informational text","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"How does the section “Destruction and Memory” contribute to the text?","o":["It describes the daily life of ordinary people in the first Saudi state.","It explains how the site declined yet remained historically recognised.","It explains how modern museums were built inside the ruins.","It compares Diriyah with other UNESCO sites in the Kingdom."],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes how specific sections contribute to meaning","b":"221–230","g":"IT"}],"set":"3"},{"p":3,"goal":"IT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"What is the central idea of the text?","o":["Synthetic clothing is the main source of all plastic pollution in the oceans.","Microplastics are a serious and fully proven cause of illness in most people around the world, so plastic must be banned.","Microplastics are now widespread, their health effects are uncertain, and reducing plastic use is a key solution.","Scientists have found microplastics only in the deep ocean and in Arctic ice."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines main/central idea in informational text","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"How does the second paragraph develop the central idea?","o":["by explaining the different sources of microplastics","by defining the size of a microplastic","by listing the countries that have already banned microbeads","by describing the health effects on people"],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes how specific paragraphs contribute to meaning","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"Why can microplastics move “up the food chain”?","o":["Rain lifts them into the air and spreads them.","They float slowly upward from the ocean floor to the surface.","Birds carry them from the sea to the land.","Small animals eat them and are then eaten by larger animals."],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes implicit relationships between ideas","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":4,"t":"mc","s":"Which statement about microplastics is supported by the text?","o":["All of them come from bottles and bags left on beaches.","Some are released directly from clothing fibres and tyres.","They have been shown to cause disease in humans.","They are larger than five centimetres in size."],"k":"B","sk":"Identifies evidence that supports a statement","b":"221–230","g":"IT"}],"set":"4"},{"p":4,"goal":"VO","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"The roots **phil** (“love”) and **anthrop** (“human”) combine in **philanthropist**. A philanthropist is someone who —","o":["gives money or time to help other people","loves to collect rare old books","studies the long history of human beings","designs buildings for large cities"],"k":"A","sk":"Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots","b":"221–230","g":"VO"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"Which word expresses the most CRITICAL view of a person who spends little money?","o":["thrifty","careful","economical","miserly"],"k":"D","sk":"Understands precise connotations of words","b":"221–230","g":"VO"}],"set":"5"},{"p":null,"goal":"VO","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"The root **bene** means “good.” A **benign** tumour is one that is —","o":["growing quickly","difficult to find","very large","not harmful"],"k":"D","sk":"Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots","b":"221–230","g":"VO"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"Which word suggests the MOST extreme degree of tiredness?","o":["drowsy","depleted","sleepy","weary"],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes nuances in meaning among related words","b":"221–230","g":"VO"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"“Opening the investigation was like opening **Pandora’s box**.” In a Greek myth, Pandora opened a box that released all the world’s troubles. What does the allusion suggest?","o":["The investigation released many unexpected problems.","The investigation uncovered a valuable hidden treasure.","The investigation was quick and simple.","The investigation was about Greek history."],"k":"A","sk":"Interprets allusion in context","b":"221–230","g":"VO"}],"set":"6"},{"p":5,"goal":"LT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"How does the opening stage direction create dramatic irony?","o":["The audience is told that the judge is secretly a science teacher at the school.","The audience knows that Salem will lose the case.","The audience knows the true cause of the damage while the prosecutor does not.","The audience learns that the defendant failed his science test."],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes dramatic irony","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"What does Hamad’s statement “The case is simple” reveal about him?","o":["He has reached a conclusion too quickly and with too much confidence.","He wants the trial to end because he is bored.","He secretly believes the defendant is innocent.","He has carefully and patiently considered all of the possible evidence."],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes dialogue to understand characters","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"What is the effect of Salem’s question about the weather?","o":["It changes the subject to avoid the prosecutor’s points.","It shifts attention to overlooked evidence and builds tension.","It shows that Salem does not really understand the case at all.","It proves immediately that the defendant was not there."],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes how dialogue advances plot","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":4,"t":"mc","s":"What tone does the judge’s final line express?","o":["joking and light-hearted","angry and accusing","confused and uncertain","reflective and instructive"],"k":"D","sk":"Determines tone in literary text","b":"221–230","g":"LT"}],"set":"7"},{"p":6,"goal":"LT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"What is an elegy, as suggested by the poem’s title and content?","o":["a poem that tells a funny story","a poem that gives instructions","a poem that celebrates a victory","a poem that mourns something lost"],"k":"D","sk":"Identifies the features of a poetic form","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"What contrast is developed in the first stanza?","o":["between the speaker’s town and a neighbouring city","between the past winters and present summers","between the warm, handmade old souq and the cold, polished new building","between the spice-sellers in the old souq and the customers who visit them"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes how specific parts of a poem contribute to meaning","b":"221–230","g":"LT"}],"set":"8"},{"p":7,"goal":"IT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"What is the speaker’s main purpose?","o":["to encourage graduates to treat failure as a step toward growth","to explain how to succeed in mathematics at university","to describe the speaker’s own career in detail","to warn graduates that most of them will fail their first university exams"],"k":"A","sk":"Determines author’s purpose","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the speaker include the anecdote about failing a mathematics exam?","o":["to prove that mathematics is the most difficult subject that students take","to build credibility and show that failure need not end a future","to make the audience laugh at the beginning","to criticise the teachers who marked the exam"],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes rhetorical devices: anecdote","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"“…holding the result in my pocket as if it could burn through the fabric.” What does this simile convey?","o":["the speaker’s fear that the paper would be lost","the speaker’s excitement about the result","the intense shame and anxiety the speaker felt","the hot weather on the day the results came out"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes the effect of figurative language","b":"221–230","g":"IT"}],"set":"9"},{"p":8,"goal":"IT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"What is the author’s claim?","o":["Wealthy people should be banned from travelling abroad.","Rockets should be launched only for scientific purposes in future.","All space exploration should be stopped immediately.","Space tourism in its current form is an unjustifiable luxury."],"k":"D","sk":"Determines author’s main argument or claim","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"Which word in the final sentence reveals the author’s attitude toward space tourists?","o":["invest","humanity","privileged","science"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes how word choice reveals viewpoint","b":"221–230","g":"IT"}],"set":"10"},{"p":9,"goal":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Which information from Text 2 helps explain what Huda checks in Text 1?","o":["Brine must be managed carefully to protect marine life.","Underground aquifers are being used faster than they refill.","Gulf countries have very few rivers and only limited rainfall each year.","Most modern plants force sea water through membranes that block salt."],"k":"D","sk":"Synthesizes information found in multiple texts","b":"221–230","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"How does Text 1’s treatment of desalination DIFFER from Text 2’s?","o":["Text 1 gives statistics; Text 2 tells a personal story.","Text 1 argues against desalination; Text 2 argues for it.","Text 1 focuses on the human effort behind the system; Text 2 explains the system and its challenges.","Text 1 explains in detail how reverse osmosis works; Text 2 describes one engineer’s long night shift."],"k":"C","sk":"Compares and contrasts multiple texts","b":"221–230","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the author of Text 1 mention “half a million people” asleep?","o":["to explain why the alarm keeps going off","to emphasise how many people depend on unseen work","to prove that people waste too much water","to show that the plant is too small for the city"],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes how specific details contribute to meaning","b":"221–230","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"},{"n":4,"t":"mc","s":"Which idea is supported by BOTH texts?","o":["Reliable drinking water in the region depends on complex, continuous work.","Desalination plants have become far too expensive for the region to continue running.","Engineers should be paid more than other workers.","Rainfall in the Gulf is increasing every year."],"k":"A","sk":"Determines a shared idea in multiple texts","b":"221–230","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"}],"set":"11"},{"p":10,"goal":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"“Round as moons, cheap as dates.” What does the merchant’s phrase emphasise?","o":["that dates had become more valuable than pearls","that cultured pearls were perfect in shape and very inexpensive","that the grandfather’s pearls were large and round","that pearls should only be sold at night when the full moon is out"],"k":"B","sk":"Interprets simile in context","b":"221–230","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"Which idea is shared by BOTH texts?","o":["Pearl diving was a safe, easy and well-paid job for most families on the coast.","Natural pearls are still the Gulf’s main export.","The collapse of pearling forced deep changes in people’s working lives.","The oil industry failed soon after it began."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines a shared idea in multiple texts","b":"221–230","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"}],"set":"12"}]},{"id":"R9","n":9,"title":"RIT 9","goal":"RIT","band":"231–240","skills":["Understands how characters are developed or changed","Analyzes the development of theme in literary text","Summarizes informational text","Interprets paradox in context"],"vocab":[],"sets":[{"p":0,"goal":"LT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"How does Yasir’s final request show a change in him?","o":["He chooses to connect with his father through something they can share.","He wants to fix the model because it has been damaged.","He wants to prove that he can build better windows.","He decides that he wants to become an architect like his father one day."],"k":"A","sk":"Understands how characters are developed or changed","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"Which statement BEST expresses a theme of the story?","o":["Scale models are the best way to remember a family home.","Children should forgive their parents for anything they do.","Successful careers always damage family relationships beyond any hope of repair.","Understanding another person’s regrets can open a path to reconciliation."],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes the development of theme in literary text","b":"231–240","g":"LT"}],"set":"1"},{"p":1,"goal":"LT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the author use short, fragmented sentences such as “The pump. Also on the list.”?","o":["to show that Murad is far too angry with the guest to speak or think clearly","to show Murad’s thoughts quickly recognising the truth in the criticism","to list the items that the guest stole from the room","to describe the noises made by the old pump"],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes how sentence structure contributes to meaning","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"Why does Murad leave the harsh entry in the book?","o":["It now represents a lesson that led to real improvements.","He forgot about it after fixing the heater.","His mother has forbidden him from ever touching the book again.","He wants future guests to know the rooms were cold."],"k":"A","sk":"Understands how characters are developed or changed","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"How does the final guest’s entry contribute to the story?","o":["It proves that the first guest was lying about the cold rooms and the noisy pump.","It introduces a new problem for the family to solve.","It echoes the harsh review point by point, showing the change it caused.","It proves that the first guest was lying about the rooms."],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes how an ending contributes to meaning","b":"231–240","g":"LT"}],"set":"2"},{"p":2,"goal":"IT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"What “difficult balance” do restorers face, according to the text?","o":["using modern materials while hiding them from visitors","protecting the ruins while moving them to Riyadh","welcoming visitors while keeping the site authentic","rebuilding quickly while keeping costs as low as possible"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes implicit relationships between ideas","b":"231–240","g":"IT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"Which is the BEST objective summary of the text?","o":["At-Turaif in Diriyah, centre of the first Saudi state from 1727, was destroyed in 1818, listed by UNESCO in 2010 and is now being restored with care for authenticity.","Diriyah is the most beautiful and important place in Saudi Arabia, and everyone should visit it at least once to see the old palaces and walk through its narrow streets.","In 1727, Imam Muhammad bin Saud became ruler of Diriyah, a town beside Wadi Hanifa on the edge of modern Riyadh.","Restorers in Diriyah use traditional methods to rebuild walls, and museums now tell visitors the story of the district."],"k":"A","sk":"Summarizes informational text","b":"231–240","g":"IT"}],"set":"3"},{"p":3,"goal":"IT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"What does the author’s choice of the word “caution” in the third paragraph signal?","o":["careful uncertainty rather than alarm about health effects","complete confidence that microplastics are entirely harmless","strong anger at companies that make plastic","disappointment that research has stopped"],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes how word choice affects tone","b":"231–240","g":"IT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"What is the author’s purpose in the final paragraph?","o":["to describe how plastic is manufactured","to criticise countries that have not banned microbeads","to present a range of responses and highlight the most effective one","to argue that new technology alone can solve the whole problem of pollution"],"k":"C","sk":"Determines specific purpose of a paragraph","b":"231–240","g":"IT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"Which is the BEST objective summary of the text?","o":["Microplastics are terrifying, and everyone should immediately stop buying anything that contains plastic, because scientists have proved that they cause serious illness in most people.","Microplastics form when plastic breaks down or is shed from products, spread through the food chain with uncertain health effects, and may be reduced mainly by using less plastic.","Pieces of plastic smaller than five millimetres have been found in the Arctic, in soil and in the air.","Engineers are designing washing-machine filters that catch tiny fibres from synthetic clothes before they reach the sea."],"k":"B","sk":"Summarizes informational text","b":"231–240","g":"IT"}],"set":"4"},{"p":4,"goal":"VO","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"“The team won the championship, but lost its three best players to injury; it was a **Pyrrhic victory**.” What does the allusion suggest?","o":["The win was unfair because of the injured players.","The win came at such a high cost that it was barely worth it.","The win was unexpected and surprised everyone.","The win was achieved very easily and without any real cost to the team."],"k":"B","sk":"Interprets allusion in context","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"Which sentence contains a paradox?","o":["“I have told you this a million times already.”","“The old library was as quiet as a desert on a winter night.”","“The more you learn, the more you realise how little you know.”","“The wind howled angrily around the walls.”"],"k":"C","sk":"Interprets paradox in context","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"Read the paragraph. “At first the plan seemed **innocuous**. It changed only one rule, affected few students and cost nothing. Only later did its harmful effects become clear.” What does **innocuous** mean?","o":["harmless","expensive","complicated","popular"],"k":"A","sk":"Uses paragraph-level context to determine word meaning","b":"231–240","g":"VO"}],"set":"5"},{"p":null,"goal":"VO","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Read the paragraph. “The manager’s praise was **perfunctory**. She said ‘good job’ without looking up from her phone, and moved on before anyone could reply.” What does **perfunctory** mean?","o":["warm, enthusiastic and very detailed","surprising and unexpected","done without real interest or care","angry and critical"],"k":"C","sk":"Uses paragraph-level context to determine word meaning","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"In which sentence does **conviction** mean “a firm belief”?","o":["She spoke with such conviction that everyone listened.","His conviction for theft was finally overturned on appeal.","The court’s conviction was announced on Monday.","After the conviction, the case was closed."],"k":"A","sk":"Determines which meaning of a multiple-meaning word fits","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"Which analogy is CORRECT? **Ephemeral** is to **lasting** as **scarce** is to —","o":["precious","rare","missing","abundant"],"k":"D","sk":"Uses analogies to understand word relationships","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":4,"t":"mc","s":"“The quietest people often have the loudest minds.” Which device does this sentence use?","o":["alliteration","onomatopoeia","paradox","simile"],"k":"C","sk":"Interprets paradox in context","b":"231–240","g":"VO"}],"set":"6"},{"p":5,"goal":"LT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"How do the stage directions *(Silence. HAMAD looks at his notes, then at the photograph.)* affect the pacing?","o":["They introduce a new character who changes the verdict.","They slow the scene down at the turning point, letting the impact sink in.","They show that the photographs have been lost.","They speed the scene up so that the trial can end quickly and everyone can leave."],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes how structure and pacing create effects","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"What is the central theme of the scene?","o":["Prosecutors should never withdraw a charge.","Mock trials are the best way to teach science.","Storms cause much more damage to school buildings than students ever do.","Assuming we already know the truth can lead to unjust conclusions."],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes the development of theme in literary text","b":"231–240","g":"LT"}],"set":"7"},{"p":6,"goal":"LT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"What tone does the phrase “called it progress” create?","o":["anger at the spice-sellers for leaving the souq","quiet irony and doubt about whether the change was truly progress","pure celebration of the town’s modern development and its new shops","confusion about what the word progress means"],"k":"B","sk":"Determines tone in poetry","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"“Its fountains sing a borrowed song.” What does this suggest about the mall?","o":["Its beauty feels imported and lacks local character.","Its fountains play music from famous singers.","Its fountains are broken and make strange noises.","Its designers borrowed money to build it."],"k":"A","sk":"Interprets personification in context","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the second stanza run into the third in the middle of the father’s sentence?","o":["to show that the father is talking to a stranger","to show that the mall is closing for the night","to mirror how his memory spills over and breaks his composure","to show that the poet simply ran out of space on the page while writing"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes how the organization or form of a poem affects its meaning","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":4,"t":"mc","s":"What does the father’s uncertainty “Right here — or here — I can’t be sure” reveal?","o":["The physical traces of the past are gone, though the memory remains.","The father is trying to find a shop that sells spices.","The father never visited the old souq in his youth.","The father is lost in the new building and cannot find the mall’s exit."],"k":"A","sk":"Makes inferences from poetry","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":5,"t":"mc","s":"What is the theme of the poem?","o":["Shopping malls are much more comfortable places to shop than old markets.","Modern change can erase places that hold a community’s memories.","Cardamom is the most important spice in Arabian cooking.","Fathers should not take their children to old places."],"k":"B","sk":"Determines theme in poetry","b":"231–240","g":"LT"}],"set":"8"},{"p":7,"goal":"IT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Which line uses parallelism MOST clearly?","o":["“Graduates, families, teachers: today you are celebrating an ending.”","“It had simply changed direction.”","“Some will tell you that success is about talent.”","“So aspire high. Work hard. Fail well.”"],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes rhetorical devices: parallelism","b":"231–240","g":"IT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"How does the speaker address the view that success is about talent?","o":["by rejecting the idea of talent completely as a myth that holds people back","by changing the subject to the graduates’ families","by accepting that talent matters but arguing that effort can matter more","by agreeing that talent is the only thing that matters"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes how an author acknowledges and responds to conflicting views","b":"231–240","g":"IT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"What is the effect of the rhetorical question “Which will you be?”","o":["It challenges each listener to decide how he will respond to life.","It introduces a new topic that the speech will explain.","It asks the audience for information the speaker does not know.","It shows that the speaker is uncertain of his own view."],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes rhetorical devices: rhetorical questions","b":"231–240","g":"IT"}],"set":"9"},{"p":8,"goal":"IT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"How does the author qualify the environmental argument?","o":["by admitting that scientists are still studying the effects","by citing exact measurements of the damage caused to the atmosphere","by comparing rockets with cars and aeroplanes","by claiming that all rockets will soon be banned"],"k":"A","sk":"Evaluates the soundness of reasoning","b":"231–240","g":"IT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"What type of appeal does the second reason mainly use?","o":["an appeal to humour","an appeal to fairness and moral values","an appeal to scientific facts and statistics","an appeal to expert authority"],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes rhetorical appeals","b":"231–240","g":"IT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"How does the author respond to the argument about aviation?","o":["by denying that aviation was ever only a luxury enjoyed by the wealthy few","by conceding the comparison but distinguishing aviation’s practical uses","by agreeing that space tourism will become cheap soon","by ignoring the comparison completely"],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes how an author acknowledges and responds to conflicting views","b":"231–240","g":"IT"},{"n":4,"t":"mc","s":"What is the effect of the short sentences “Exploration is noble. Joyrides are not.”?","o":["They show that the author secretly admires space tourists and their courage.","They introduce a new counterclaim to be discussed.","They suggest that the author is unsure of the argument.","They sharpen the contrast between valuable exploration and mere entertainment."],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes rhetorical devices: parallelism and contrast","b":"231–240","g":"IT"},{"n":5,"t":"mc","s":"Which evidence would MOST strengthen the first reason?","o":["peer-reviewed measurements of the atmospheric effects of rocket launches","the ticket price of a single seat on a commercial space flight for one tourist","an interview with a famous space tourist","a photograph of the Earth taken from space"],"k":"A","sk":"Evaluates evidence used to support claims","b":"231–240","g":"IT"}],"set":"10"},{"p":9,"goal":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"What does Huda mean by “That is the point”?","o":["She plans to stop working night shifts soon.","She is frustrated that nobody ever thanks engineers for their hard work.","She wants the public to learn about desalination.","The success of her work is that people never have to notice it."],"k":"D","sk":"Determines the meaning of a nonliteral phrase","b":"231–240","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"Which challenge mentioned in Text 2 is NOT shown in Text 1?","o":["the need for plants to run reliably every day","the need to manage brine to protect marine life","the dependence of people on desalinated water","the importance of maintaining the membranes"],"k":"B","sk":"Compares or contrasts details in multiple texts","b":"231–240","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"}],"set":"11"},{"p":10,"goal":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Which information from Text 2 explains the merchant’s low offer in Text 1?","o":["Many divers found new work in construction and oil fields.","Pearl diving had been the backbone of the economy for generations.","Divers went down with little more than a nose clip, a rope and a heavy stone weight.","Cultured pearls from Japan caused prices for natural pearls to fall sharply."],"k":"D","sk":"Synthesizes information found in multiple texts","b":"231–240","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"What does the grandfather’s silence about the sea suggest?","o":["His loss is too deep to talk about, though it remains with him.","He prefers working on building sites to diving.","He has forgotten almost everything about his many years as a pearl diver.","He is angry with his family for leaving the coast."],"k":"A","sk":"Makes inferences from literary text","b":"231–240","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"How does Text 1’s treatment of the decline DIFFER from Text 2’s?","o":["Text 1 explains the economic causes of the decline; Text 2 shows its emotional cost.","Text 1 is about Japan; Text 2 is about Bahrain.","Text 1 argues the decline was good; Text 2 argues it was bad.","Text 1 shows its personal, emotional cost; Text 2 explains its economic causes."],"k":"D","sk":"Compares and contrasts multiple texts","b":"231–240","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"},{"n":4,"t":"mc","s":"Which factor mentioned in Text 2 is NOT reflected in Text 1?","o":["the global economic depression of the 1930s","the arrival of cultured pearls from Japan","divers moving into construction work","the rise of the oil industry"],"k":"A","sk":"Compares or contrasts details in multiple texts","b":"231–240","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"},{"n":5,"t":"mc","s":"What does the detail that the pouch stayed in a drawer “for forty years” suggest?","o":["The grandfather simply forgot that he had kept the pearls for so long.","The pearls became more valuable with time.","The grandfather held on to his past even after it lost its value.","The family could not find a buyer for forty years."],"k":"C","sk":"Interprets symbolism in literary text","b":"231–240","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"}],"set":"12"}]}],"mocks":[{"id":"A","note":"Full practice test · 43 questions","sections":[{"passage":{"title":"The Bus Driver","genre":"story","paras":["Every morning, the same driver took Route 12 through the old neighbourhood: a grey-haired man who hummed under his breath and greeted every passenger by name. Talal found it irritating. “He drives like a tortoise,” he complained to his friend. “And why does he care what our names are?”","One morning, a boy at the back was struggling with a mathematics worksheet. The driver, stopped at a red light, glanced in the mirror. “The second one,” he said without turning round. “You’ve multiplied when you should have divided.” The boy checked. He was right.","Talal stared. At the next stop, he asked the old man, half joking, “Were you a teacher?”","“For thirty-one years,” the driver said. “Mathematics. When I retired, the house was too quiet.” He pulled away from the stop slowly, smoothly. “Teaching and driving are not so different,” he added. “Everyone is going somewhere. My job is to get them there safely, even if some of them are in a hurry.”","The next morning, when the bus arrived, Talal said, “Good morning, Ustadh.” The old man smiled and said Talal’s name, as he always had."]},"items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"What is Talal’s attitude toward the driver at the beginning?","o":["impatient and dismissive","curious and friendly","frightened and nervous","grateful and respectful"],"k":"A","sk":"Infers character feelings","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"Which event leads Talal to see the driver differently?","o":["The driver drives faster than usual to reach the school on time.","The driver spots a mistake on a student’s worksheet from the front of the bus.","The driver refuses to let Talal’s friend onto the bus.","The driver arrives late at the school because of heavy traffic in the neighbourhood that morning."],"k":"B","sk":"Identifies the turning point","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"What does the driver mean by “Everyone is going somewhere”?","o":["He wants to retire from driving as well as teaching.","The bus route is longer than most people realise.","All of the passengers on the bus are late for school or work every morning.","Like students, passengers are all on journeys that he helps them complete."],"k":"D","sk":"Interprets metaphor in context","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":4,"t":"mc","s":"Which theme does the story develop?","o":["Students should always do their homework at home.","Retired teachers should not be allowed to drive school buses in the city.","People we judge quickly often have lives and value we do not see.","Slow drivers are safer than fast ones on busy roads."],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes the development of theme in literary text","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":5,"t":"mc","s":"Why is it significant that the driver says Talal’s name “as he always had”?","o":["It shows that Talal has changed buses to a new route.","It shows that the driver is still angry about Talal’s comment.","It shows that the driver’s kindness never depended on Talal’s respect.","It shows that the driver has finally learned Talal’s name after many months."],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes how specific details contribute to meaning","b":"231–240","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"Lithium: The Metal in Your Pocket","genre":"informational","paras":["The phone in your pocket, the laptop on your desk and the electric car on the street all have something in common: they depend on batteries made with lithium, the lightest of all metals.","Lithium is valuable for batteries because it stores a great deal of energy for its weight. Lithium-ion batteries can be recharged hundreds of times, which made portable electronics practical and is now helping to make electric vehicles common.","But obtaining lithium has costs. Much of the world’s supply comes from two sources: hard-rock mines, and salt flats in South America where lithium-rich water is pumped into large ponds and left to evaporate. In dry regions, this process uses large amounts of water, which can affect local farmers and ecosystems.","As demand rises, researchers are working on two fronts: finding cleaner ways to extract lithium, and improving recycling so that lithium from old batteries can be used again. The future of clean energy, it seems, depends not only on new technology, but on how responsibly we obtain the materials it needs."]},"items":[{"n":6,"t":"mc","s":"According to the text, why is lithium especially useful in batteries?","o":["It stores a lot of energy for its weight.","It can be mined without any cost.","It never needs to be recharged.","It is the most common metal found on Earth."],"k":"A","sk":"Locates details in informational text","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":7,"t":"mc","s":"What is the author’s purpose in the third paragraph?","o":["to describe the history of mining in South America","to argue that electric cars should be banned","to explain exactly how lithium-ion batteries are recharged each night","to show that lithium production has environmental and social costs"],"k":"D","sk":"Determines specific purpose of a paragraph","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":8,"t":"mc","s":"Why could lithium extraction in dry regions affect local farmers?","o":["Mining companies buy all the farmland near the salt flats.","The evaporation method uses large amounts of scarce water.","Batteries are stored on farms after they are recycled.","Lithium makes the soil too salty for most crops."],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes implicit relationships between ideas","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":9,"t":"mc","s":"How is the text mainly structured?","o":["problem, then a single solution, then results","benefits, then costs, then possible solutions","chronological history of battery invention","comparison of lithium with other metals"],"k":"B","sk":"Describes the organizational structure of a text","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":10,"t":"mc","s":"What does the final sentence suggest about the author’s point of view?","o":["Lithium batteries should be replaced by older technology.","New technology will solve all of our problems without any effort from us.","Recycling batteries is too difficult to be worthwhile.","Clean energy must be pursued responsibly, not only enthusiastically."],"k":"D","sk":"Determines author’s viewpoint","b":"231–240","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"The Vizier and the Weaver","genre":"folk tale","paras":["A proud vizier once ordered a weaver to make him a carpet “finer than any in the kingdom.” The weaver worked for a year. When the carpet was finished, its colours were deep and its patterns so delicate that people came from far away to see it.","The vizier examined it closely and found, in one corner, a single thread of the wrong colour. “You have ruined it,” he declared, and refused to pay.","The weaver bowed. “My lord,” he said, “that thread is the only one in the carpet that is mine. The other ten thousand belong to the pattern. That one reminds me that I am a man and not the pattern-maker of the world.”","The vizier laughed scornfully and left. That year he made many decisions without listening to anyone, certain that he could not be wrong. Within two years, his mistakes had cost him the sultan’s trust, and he was dismissed.","On his last day in the palace, he passed the weaver’s carpet, now hanging in the great hall. He stopped before the single wrong thread and stood there for a long time."]},"items":[{"n":11,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the vizier refuse to pay the weaver?","o":["People came from far away to see the carpet.","The finished carpet is much smaller than he had ordered from the weaver.","He finds a single thread of the wrong colour in the carpet.","The weaver took more than a year to finish."],"k":"C","sk":"Locates details about plot","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":12,"t":"mc","s":"What does the weaver’s explanation of the thread reveal?","o":["He deliberately included an imperfection as a reminder of humility.","He ran out of the correct colour of thread.","He wants the vizier to pay him twice the price.","He made a careless mistake in the pattern and is now trying to hide it."],"k":"A","sk":"Interprets symbolism in literary text","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":13,"t":"mc","s":"What is the main message of the tale?","o":["Pride that refuses to admit imperfection leads to failure.","Rulers should hang carpets in their great halls.","Weavers should always be paid in full before they begin work.","Carpets are more valuable than political power."],"k":"A","sk":"Determines the moral of a folk tale","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":14,"t":"mc","s":"How does the vizier’s fate connect to the weaver’s lesson?","o":["His love of fine carpets leads him to waste the sultan’s money.","His refusal to admit he could be wrong leads to his downfall.","His decision not to pay causes the weaver to complain.","His friendship with the weaver makes the sultan suspicious."],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes how events develop theme","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":15,"t":"mc","s":"What does the final image of the vizier standing before the thread suggest?","o":["He plans to buy the carpet from the palace.","He is looking for more mistakes in the pattern.","He is still angry about the flaw in the carpet.","He finally understands the lesson about humility."],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes how an ending contributes to meaning","b":"231–240","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"Snow on Jabal Al-Lawz","genre":"poem","paras":["We drove all night through Tabuk’s dark","Because the radio had said","That snow had fallen — snow! — a mark","Of white upon the mountain’s head.","","And there it was: a thin, cold cloth","Thrown over stone that never sleeps,","As light as flour, as soft as moth,","Already melting into streams.","","My father, who had never seen","A single flake in fifty years,","Stood laughing like a boy, between","The patient rocks, and wiped his tears."],"poem":true},"items":[{"n":16,"t":"mc","s":"What does the repetition “snow had fallen — snow!” convey?","o":["the cold temperature inside the car","the speaker’s fear of driving in bad weather","the family’s excitement and disbelief at the news","the radio presenter’s confusion about the forecast"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes the effect of repetition and punctuation","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":17,"t":"mc","s":"“A thin, cold cloth / Thrown over stone that never sleeps.” What does this metaphor suggest about the snow?","o":["It has been there for many years without melting.","It is thick enough to bury the ancient mountain completely.","It is a light, temporary covering on the ancient mountain.","It is dirty and grey from the dust of the road."],"k":"C","sk":"Interprets metaphor in context","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":18,"t":"mc","s":"What does the father’s reaction in the final stanza reveal?","o":["embarrassment at crying in front of his whole family in the car on the mountain","tiredness after driving through the night","disappointment that there was so little snow","joy and deep emotion at an experience he had waited a lifetime for"],"k":"D","sk":"Determines details that reveal characters’ feelings","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":19,"t":"mc","s":"Why might the poet include the detail “Already melting into streams”?","o":["to suggest that the weather report was wrong","to emphasise how brief and precious the sight is","to explain where the mountain’s rivers come from","to show that the family arrived too late to see it"],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes how specific details contribute to meaning","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":20,"t":"mc","s":"How does the poem’s focus shift across the three stanzas?","o":["from the journey, to the snow, to the father’s response","from joy, to sadness, to anger","from the father, to the high mountain, to the car radio","from morning, to afternoon, to night"],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes how the organization of a poem affects its meaning","b":"231–240","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"The Juniper Forests of the South","genre":"informational","paras":["Most people do not picture forests when they think of Saudi Arabia. Yet the high mountains of the south-west, in regions such as Asir and Al-Baha, are home to woodlands of African juniper, evergreen trees that can live for hundreds of years.","These forests do far more than provide shade. Their roots hold soil on steep slopes, reducing erosion. Their branches catch moisture from mountain fog, helping to recharge underground water. They also shelter birds, insects and mammals found in few other places in the Kingdom.","In recent decades, however, scientists have observed many junipers dying back, especially at lower altitudes. Their branches turn brown from the top downward. Researchers point to several possible causes acting together: higher temperatures, changing rainfall, overgrazing by goats and camels that eat young trees, and pressure from roads and construction.","Because juniper seedlings grow slowly, recovery takes time. Conservation efforts include protecting areas from grazing, collecting seeds and growing young trees in nurseries before planting them on the mountains. Scientists stress that these forests, once lost, could take generations to return."]},"items":[{"n":21,"t":"mc","s":"What is the central idea of the text?","o":["Goats and camels are the main reason why trees die in the mountains.","Juniper trees can live for hundreds of years if they are planted in nurseries.","Saudi Arabia has many more forests than most people realise, especially in the far north of the country.","Southern juniper forests are ecologically valuable but threatened, and their recovery will be slow."],"k":"D","sk":"Determines main/central idea in informational text","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":22,"t":"mc","s":"How does the second paragraph develop the central idea?","o":["by explaining why the forests matter to soil, water and wildlife","by describing in detail the many different causes of the forests’ decline","by explaining how seedlings are grown in nurseries","by comparing junipers with trees in other countries"],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes how specific paragraphs contribute to meaning","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":23,"t":"mc","s":"Why does protecting areas from grazing help the forests?","o":["Animals break the roots that hold the soil on slopes.","Animals drink the water that the trees collect from fog.","Animals eat young trees that are needed for the forests to regrow.","Animals bring diseases that slowly turn the green branches brown."],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes implicit relationships between ideas","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":24,"t":"mc","s":"What does the phrase “several possible causes acting together” suggest about the scientists’ view?","o":["The causes of the decline are not understood at all.","The decline is complex and cannot be blamed on a single factor.","The trees are dying only because of construction.","Scientists have identified the one main cause of the problem."],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes how word choice advances purpose","b":"231–240","g":"IT"},{"n":25,"t":"mc","s":"What is the purpose of the final sentence?","o":["to explain how long juniper trees can live","to predict that the forests will recover quickly","to stress the urgency of protecting the forests now","to suggest that conservation efforts have failed"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes the purpose of an ending","b":"231–240","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"Average Commute Times","genre":"chart","paras":["**Average one-way commute time to school or work (minutes), by transport type — survey of 400 residents**","Private car: 38 · Bus: 52 · Metro: 31 · Walking: 14 · Bicycle: 19","Note: Most people who walk or cycle live within 3 km of their destination. Car journeys were measured during peak hours."]},"items":[{"n":26,"t":"mc","s":"Which form of transport has the longest average commute?","o":["bicycle","bus","metro","private car"],"k":"B","sk":"Interprets information in charts or graphs","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":27,"t":"mc","s":"What is the purpose of the note below the data?","o":["to give information needed to interpret the figures fairly","to advertise the many health benefits of walking and cycling in the city","to explain how the survey was paid for","to list the routes used by the city’s buses"],"k":"A","sk":"Determines specific purpose of a text feature","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":28,"t":"mc","s":"How much time does a metro user save each day, compared with a car user, if both make one return trip?","o":["7 minutes","21 minutes","28 minutes","14 minutes"],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes quantitative information in informational text","b":"231–240","g":"IT"},{"n":29,"t":"mc","s":"Why would it be misleading to conclude that walking is the fastest way to travel?","o":["Walkers do not have to wait at traffic lights.","Walkers usually live much closer to their destinations.","Very few people walk to school or work.","Walking times in the survey were measured only at night."],"k":"B","sk":"Evaluates reasoning about data","b":"231–240","g":"IT"},{"n":30,"t":"mc","s":"Which claim is BEST supported by the data?","o":["Buses are always much slower than private cars in every single part of the city.","Most residents walk or cycle to their destinations.","For people near metro lines, the metro is faster than driving at peak hours.","Everyone should cycle instead of driving to work."],"k":"C","sk":"Evaluates evidence used to support claims","b":"231–240","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":null,"items":[{"n":31,"t":"mc","s":"The root **morph** means “shape.” A **metamorphosis** is —","o":["a complete change of form","a shape with many sides","a study of ancient shapes","a slow movement of rock"],"k":"A","sk":"Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots","b":"221–230","g":"VO"},{"n":32,"t":"mc","s":"Read the sentence. “The report was deliberately **ambiguous**, so each side claimed it supported their position.” What does **ambiguous** mean?","o":["extremely detailed, careful and precise","written in a foreign language","hostile to both sides equally","open to more than one interpretation"],"k":"D","sk":"Uses context to determine word meaning","b":"221–230","g":"VO"},{"n":33,"t":"mc","s":"“Everything the businessman touched turned to gold; he had the **Midas touch**.” What does the allusion mean?","o":["He was greedy and lost his fortune.","He worked as a jeweller in the market.","He was very careful with fragile objects.","He was extremely successful at making money."],"k":"D","sk":"Interprets allusion in context","b":"221–230","g":"VO"},{"n":34,"t":"mc","s":"Which word BEST completes the sentence? “The new law was intended to reduce pollution; ___, it has led to a sharp rise in the cost of transport.”","o":["therefore","specifically","however","similarly"],"k":"C","sk":"Identifies words in context that show a contrast","b":"221–230","g":"VO"},{"n":35,"t":"mc","s":"The root **log** means “word” or “study.” A **monologue** is —","o":["a long speech by one person","a conversation between two people","the study of ancient languages","a book that lists word meanings"],"k":"A","sk":"Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots","b":"221–230","g":"VO"},{"n":36,"t":"mc","s":"“After the team lost 9–0, the coach said, ‘We could have played slightly better.’” What device is the coach using?","o":["personification","understatement","alliteration","hyperbole"],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes figurative language: understatement","b":"221–230","g":"VO"},{"n":37,"t":"mc","s":"Which word suggests the STRONGEST disapproval?","o":["question","criticise","condemn","doubt"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes nuances in meaning among related words","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":38,"t":"mc","s":"In which sentence does **sanction** mean “a penalty”?","o":["Several countries imposed trade sanctions on the regime.","The new rule was sanctioned by the board.","The plan has the full sanction of the committee.","The principal sanctioned the class trip to the science museum last week."],"k":"A","sk":"Determines which meaning of a multiple-meaning word fits","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":39,"t":"mc","s":"Read the paragraph. “The minister’s response was **evasive**. Asked about the delays, he spoke about the weather; asked about the costs, he praised the workers.” What does **evasive** mean?","o":["angry, threatening and very loud","avoiding giving a direct answer","detailed and honest","humble and apologetic"],"k":"B","sk":"Uses paragraph-level context to determine word meaning","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":40,"t":"mc","s":"Which word has the most POSITIVE connotation?","o":["stubborn","obstinate","rigid","steadfast"],"k":"D","sk":"Understands precise connotations of words","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":41,"t":"mc","s":"“I must be cruel only to be kind.” What kind of figurative language is this?","o":["onomatopoeia","paradox","hyperbole","simile"],"k":"B","sk":"Interprets paradox in context","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":42,"t":"mc","s":"Which analogy is CORRECT? **Empathy** is to **compassion** as **apathy** is to —","o":["kindness","curiosity","indifference","anger"],"k":"C","sk":"Uses analogies to understand word relationships","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":43,"t":"mc","s":"The prefix **pre-** means “before.” What does it mean to **preclude** something?","o":["to finish it early","to include it right at the very beginning","to repeat it several times","to prevent it from happening in advance"],"k":"D","sk":"Uses a defined root and/or affix to determine word meaning","b":"231–240","g":"VO"}]}]},{"id":"B","note":"Full practice test · 43 questions","sections":[{"passage":{"title":"The Chess Clock","genre":"story","paras":["In the final of the city chess championship, Abdulaziz had seven minutes left on his clock. His opponent, a calm boy from Dammam named Ibrahim, had forty.","Abdulaziz had spent too long on the opening, trying to find a brilliant move instead of a good one. Now every decision felt rushed. His hand hovered over the board. Somewhere behind him, his father coughed.","Then he remembered what his coach had said the week before, when Abdulaziz had lost a practice game the same way: “You are not playing the board. You are playing the clock. Breathe first. A good move made calmly beats a brilliant move made in panic.”","He sat back. He breathed. He looked at the whole board, not just the corner where he had been attacking. There it was: a simple move, nothing brilliant, that protected his king and freed his rook.","He played it with four minutes left. Ibrahim frowned and thought for a long time. Twenty moves later, with eleven seconds on Abdulaziz’s clock, Ibrahim offered a draw. Abdulaziz accepted. Shaking hands across the board, Ibrahim said, “You played the last part like a different person.” Abdulaziz smiled. “I think I was,” he said."]},"items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Why is Abdulaziz short of time?","o":["His clock was set incorrectly by the referee.","He arrived late to the championship final.","He spent too long searching for a brilliant move in the opening.","His opponent deliberately played very slowly to make him nervous."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines the cause of an event in literary text","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"What does the detail of his father coughing suggest?","o":["His father is ill and needs to leave the hall.","Abdulaziz is aware of the pressure of people watching.","The hall is too dusty for the players.","His father is trying to send him a secret signal about the game."],"k":"B","sk":"Makes inferences from literary text","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"What does the coach mean by “You are not playing the board. You are playing the clock.”?","o":["Abdulaziz lets time pressure, not the position, control his decisions.","Abdulaziz should simply play much faster if he wants to beat his opponent.","Abdulaziz should stop looking at the clock entirely.","Abdulaziz is breaking the rules of tournament chess."],"k":"A","sk":"Determines the meaning of a nonliteral phrase","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":4,"t":"mc","s":"How does the author slow the pace in the fourth paragraph?","o":["by introducing a flashback to Abdulaziz’s childhood","by switching to Ibrahim’s point of view","by adding a long and detailed description of the crowded hall","by using short sentences that mirror Abdulaziz calming himself"],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes how structure and pacing create effects","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":5,"t":"mc","s":"What does Abdulaziz mean when he says “I think I was” a different person?","o":["He believes his coach was secretly helping him.","He has changed from panicking to thinking clearly under pressure.","He was too tired to remember how he had played.","He was only pretending to be someone else in order to confuse Ibrahim."],"k":"B","sk":"Understands how characters are developed or changed","b":"231–240","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"The Verdict at the Fountain","genre":"drama","paras":["*(A village square with an old stone fountain. Two neighbours, ABU FAHD and ABU NAWAF, stand before the village elder, SHEIKH SALIM. A crowd watches.)*","ABU FAHD: My goats drank from this fountain for twenty years. Now he says the water belongs to him because the spring rises on his land.","ABU NAWAF: *(firmly)* It does rise on my land. I paid for the new pipe. Why should his goats drink for free?","SHEIKH SALIM: *(after listening)* Abu Nawaf, who paid for the fountain itself?","ABU NAWAF: *(hesitating)* My grandfather… and his grandfather. Together.","SHEIKH SALIM: So the spring is on your land, and the pipe is yours. But the fountain belongs to both families. *(He turns to the crowd.)* Here is my judgement: Abu Fahd will pay half the cost of the pipe. Abu Nawaf will let the water flow as it always has. And both of you will repair the fountain together, as your grandfathers built it.","*(The two men look at each other. Slowly, ABU NAWAF holds out his hand.)*"]},"items":[{"n":6,"t":"mc","s":"What is the conflict in the scene?","o":["Two neighbours dispute who has the right to use the water from a fountain.","Two neighbours argue loudly in the village square about who owns a herd of goats.","A crowd demands that the old fountain be destroyed.","A village elder refuses to make a judgement in a dispute."],"k":"A","sk":"Identifies conflict in literary text","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":7,"t":"mc","s":"Why does Sheikh Salim ask who paid for the fountain?","o":["to embarrass Abu Nawaf in front of the whole crowd","to find out which of the two families should pay for a completely new fountain","to delay his judgement because he is unsure what to do","to reveal a shared history that changes how the dispute should be seen"],"k":"D","sk":"Explains character motivation","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":8,"t":"mc","s":"What does the stage direction *(hesitating)* suggest about Abu Nawaf?","o":["He is afraid of Sheikh Salim’s anger.","He has forgotten the history of his family.","He realises that the answer weakens his claim.","He wants to leave the square quickly."],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes use of stage directions","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":9,"t":"mc","s":"How does Sheikh Salim’s judgement resolve the conflict?","o":["It forbids both families from using the fountain in future.","It orders Abu Nawaf to remove the new pipe from his land.","It shares the costs and duties so that both families keep a stake in the fountain.","It gives the fountain entirely to Abu Fahd because his goats need more water than anyone else’s."],"k":"C","sk":"Identifies events that lead to resolution","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":10,"t":"mc","s":"What theme does the scene suggest?","o":["Neighbours who argue can never become friends again.","Shared history can be a basis for fair solutions to present disputes.","Water should always belong to the person on whose land the spring rises.","Village elders should not interfere in private arguments."],"k":"B","sk":"Determines theme in literary text","b":"231–240","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"For an Old Teacher","genre":"poem","paras":["You never knew my name, I think;","I was a face in row fourteen,","One of the hundreds at the brink","Of all the things we might have been.","","You wrote one sentence on my page —","“There is a thinker here” — in red;","I kept it like a coin, an age,","Long after other words had fled.","","Now I stand where you used to stand","And look at faces, row on row,","And write, with your remembered hand,","The sentences they’ll never know","","Are keeping them, like coins, like keys,","Against the long uncertain years —","And I give thanks for fourteen, please,","And for your red ink, and my tears."],"poem":true},"items":[{"n":11,"t":"mc","s":"What does the first stanza suggest about the speaker’s place in the teacher’s classroom?","o":["He was the oldest student in the class.","He was the teacher’s favourite student.","He often caused trouble in the back row.","He was one unremarkable student among many."],"k":"D","sk":"Makes inferences from poetry","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":12,"t":"mc","s":"“I kept it like a coin.” What does this simile suggest?","o":["The teacher’s words were precious and carefully saved.","The words were small and easily lost.","The teacher paid the speaker for good work.","The speaker later sold his old notebook to a collector for money."],"k":"A","sk":"Interprets simile in context","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":13,"t":"mc","s":"How does the speaker’s role change in the third stanza?","o":["He has returned to row fourteen as a student.","He has become a teacher himself.","He has become the head of the school.","He has left teaching for another job."],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes how specific parts of a poem contribute to meaning","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":14,"t":"mc","s":"What is the tone of the final stanza?","o":["bitter and resentful","light-hearted and joking","anxious and uncertain","deeply grateful and moved"],"k":"D","sk":"Determines tone in poetry","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":15,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the third stanza run on into the fourth without a pause?","o":["to show how the teacher’s influence continues unbroken into new generations","to show that the poet simply forgot to add punctuation at the ends of the lines","to show that the students are not listening","to show that the speaker is tired of teaching"],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes how the organization or form of a poem affects its meaning","b":"231–240","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"Paper Comes to Baghdad","genre":"informational","paras":["For centuries, scholars in the Middle East wrote on papyrus, made from Egyptian reeds, or on parchment, made from animal skin. Papyrus was fragile, and parchment was expensive: a single large book could require the skins of many animals.","Paper-making had been developed in China centuries earlier. According to tradition, the technique reached the Islamic world in the eighth century, and by the end of that century, around 794, a paper mill was operating in Baghdad. Paper was cheaper than parchment and stronger than papyrus, and it could be made from rags and plant fibres.","The effects were far-reaching. Government offices used paper for records and letters. Booksellers’ markets grew, with shops selling copies of works on mathematics, medicine, poetry and religion. Because paper made writing cheaper, more people could own books, and knowledge could be preserved and shared on a scale that had not been possible before.","From Baghdad, paper-making spread west through Damascus and Cairo to North Africa and Spain, and eventually into Europe. Many historians argue that the scholarly achievements of this period would have been far harder without this humble material."]},"items":[{"n":16,"t":"mc","s":"What is the central idea of the text?","o":["Baghdad had the largest booksellers’ market in the world.","Papyrus and parchment were the best and most useful writing materials ever made.","The arrival of paper in Baghdad transformed how knowledge was recorded and shared.","China invented paper but never used it for important books."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines main/central idea in informational text","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":17,"t":"mc","s":"How does the first paragraph prepare the reader for the central idea?","o":["by describing how paper was first invented in China many centuries earlier","by explaining how paper is made from rags","by listing the books sold in Baghdad’s markets","by explaining the limitations of earlier writing materials"],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes the purpose of an introduction","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":18,"t":"mc","s":"Which TWO qualities made paper better than earlier materials, according to the text?","o":["It was cheaper than parchment and stronger than papyrus.","It was waterproof and could be folded easily.","It lasted longer than stone and was easier to carve.","It was lighter than papyrus and whiter than parchment."],"k":"A","sk":"Compares or contrasts details/ideas","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":19,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the author call paper a “humble material” in the final sentence?","o":["to suggest that paper was of poor quality compared with parchment and papyrus","to contrast its simple, ordinary nature with its enormous impact","to explain that paper was made by poor people","to show that scholars disliked using paper"],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes how word choice advances purpose","b":"231–240","g":"IT"},{"n":20,"t":"mc","s":"What does the phrase “According to tradition” suggest about the eighth-century date?","o":["It was recorded by the Chinese inventors of paper.","It has been proved beyond any doubt by modern scientists and historians.","It is based on accounts passed down rather than certain evidence.","It is the date when paper first reached Europe."],"k":"C","sk":"Evaluates the certainty of claims","b":"231–240","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"How People Read Books","genre":"chart","paras":["**Survey of 500 secondary-school students: format of the last book they finished**","Printed book: 230 · E-book on a phone or tablet: 145 · Audiobook: 85 · Did not finish a book this year: 40","Of the students who finished a book, those who chose printed books reported reading an average of 9 books a year; e-book readers, 7; audiobook listeners, 11."]},"items":[{"n":21,"t":"mc","s":"Which group reported reading the most books per year on average?","o":["students who did not finish a book","audiobook listeners","e-book readers","printed-book readers"],"k":"B","sk":"Interprets information in charts or graphs","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":22,"t":"mc","s":"What percentage of students had NOT finished a book this year?","o":["4%","8%","10%","40%"],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes quantitative information in informational text","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":23,"t":"mc","s":"What fraction of the students who finished a book chose printed books?","o":["about one quarter","about two thirds","about one third","exactly half"],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes quantitative information in informational text","b":"231–240","g":"IT"},{"n":24,"t":"mc","s":"Which conclusion is NOT supported by the data?","o":["Printed books were the most common format for the last book finished.","Some students did not finish any book this year.","Audiobooks make students more intelligent than printed books do.","Audiobook listeners reported reading more books on average."],"k":"C","sk":"Evaluates evidence used to support claims","b":"231–240","g":"IT"},{"n":25,"t":"mc","s":"Why should the figures for books per year be treated with some caution?","o":["They are reported by the students themselves, not measured directly.","They count only books read during school hours.","They include many students who did not finish reading any books at all.","They were collected from only five students."],"k":"A","sk":"Evaluates reasoning about data","b":"231–240","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"Close the City Centre to Cars","genre":"argument","paras":["Our historic city centre should be a place for people. Instead, it is a car park. The city should close the central district to private cars and turn its streets over to pedestrians, cyclists and public transport.","Cities that have tried this have seen clear benefits. When several European cities restricted cars in their centres, studies recorded lower air pollution, fewer road injuries and, in many cases, more visitors to local shops. People who walk past a shop are more likely to stop than people who drive past it.","A car-free centre would also protect our heritage. Vibration and pollution from heavy traffic damage old buildings, and wide roads cut through neighbourhoods that were designed for walking.","Shopkeepers often fear that closing streets to cars will drive customers away. This fear is understandable, but the evidence from many cities suggests the opposite. To ease the change, the city could add park-and-ride stations at the edge of the district and allow deliveries early in the morning.","A city centre full of people, not traffic, is not a dream. It is a choice."]},"items":[{"n":26,"t":"mc","s":"What is the author’s claim?","o":["The city should build many more car parks near the centre.","The city should move its shops to the edge of town.","The city should ban all deliveries to the centre.","The city should close its central district to private cars."],"k":"D","sk":"Determines author’s main argument or claim","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":27,"t":"mc","s":"Which evidence does the author use to support the first reason?","o":["the number of cars that enter the centre each day","a quotation from the mayor about city planning","studies from European cities showing benefits after restricting cars","a survey of the shopkeepers in the author’s own city centre about their sales"],"k":"C","sk":"Evaluates evidence used to support claims","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":28,"t":"mc","s":"Is the evidence from European cities sufficient to prove that this city will see the same results?","o":["Not fully; the cities may differ in climate, transport and habits.","Yes; the author lives in one of those European cities.","Yes; all cities respond to traffic changes in exactly the same way.","Not at all; the evidence shows that pollution increased."],"k":"A","sk":"Evaluates whether evidence is sufficient","b":"231–240","g":"IT"},{"n":29,"t":"mc","s":"How does the author address shopkeepers’ concerns?","o":["by agreeing that shops will close and accepting the loss","by suggesting that shopkeepers move to new buildings","by acknowledging the fear, citing contrary evidence and proposing practical measures","by dismissing the shopkeepers as people who do not understand how modern cities really work"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes how an author acknowledges and responds to conflicting views","b":"231–240","g":"IT"},{"n":30,"t":"mc","s":"What is the rhetorical effect of the final two sentences?","o":["They present the change as achievable and dependent on the city’s will.","They summarise the shopkeepers’ point of view.","They admit that the author’s plan is unrealistic and probably cannot work.","They introduce a new argument about tourism."],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes rhetorical devices","b":"231–240","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":null,"items":[{"n":31,"t":"mc","s":"The root **anthrop** means “human.” **Anthropology** is the study of —","o":["the stars and planets","the structure of the Earth","ancient plants and animals","human societies and cultures"],"k":"D","sk":"Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots","b":"221–230","g":"VO"},{"n":32,"t":"mc","s":"In which sentence does **gravity** mean “seriousness”?","o":["The apple fell because of gravity.","The doctor explained the gravity of the situation.","Gravity keeps the planets in orbit around the Sun.","Astronauts train to work without gravity."],"k":"B","sk":"Determines which meaning of a multiple-meaning word fits","b":"221–230","g":"VO"},{"n":33,"t":"mc","s":"Which word BEST completes the sentence? “The experiment produced surprising results; ___, the scientists decided to repeat it before publishing.”","o":["similarly","otherwise","consequently","nevertheless"],"k":"C","sk":"Words that show a cause-effect relationship","b":"221–230","g":"VO"},{"n":34,"t":"mc","s":"The prefix **circum-** means “around.” To **circumnavigate** the globe is to —","o":["fly over its poles","study its climate","draw a detailed map of it","travel all the way around it"],"k":"D","sk":"Uses a defined root and/or affix to determine word meaning","b":"221–230","g":"VO"},{"n":35,"t":"mc","s":"Which word has the most NEGATIVE connotation?","o":["notorious","renowned","famous","celebrated"],"k":"A","sk":"Understands precise connotations of words","b":"221–230","g":"VO"},{"n":36,"t":"mc","s":"“His smile was a mask.” What does this metaphor suggest?","o":["He wore a costume to the party.","His smile hid his real feelings.","He smiled at everyone he met.","His face was very pale."],"k":"B","sk":"Interprets metaphor in context","b":"221–230","g":"VO"},{"n":37,"t":"mc","s":"Read the sentence. “The new evidence did not **corroborate** the witness’s story; in fact, it contradicted it.” What does **corroborate** mean?","o":["to confirm or support","to explain in detail","to repeat word for word","to deny completely"],"k":"A","sk":"Uses context to determine word meaning","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":38,"t":"mc","s":"Which word suggests the MOST intense dislike?","o":["dislike","prefer not","abhor","object to"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes nuances in meaning among related words","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":39,"t":"mc","s":"“The company’s new product turned out to be a **white elephant**.” A white elephant is a rare animal that is costly to keep. What does the phrase suggest?","o":["The product was extremely popular with customers and sold out very quickly.","The product was small and easy to carry.","The product was designed for wildlife parks.","The product was expensive and more trouble than it was worth."],"k":"D","sk":"Interprets allusion in context","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":40,"t":"mc","s":"Read the paragraph. “The old man was known for his **taciturn** nature. At family gatherings he listened, nodded and rarely said more than a few words.” What does **taciturn** mean?","o":["easily confused","saying very little","fond of jokes","quick to anger"],"k":"B","sk":"Uses paragraph-level context to determine word meaning","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":41,"t":"mc","s":"Which analogy is CORRECT? **Benign** is to **harmless** as **malignant** is to —","o":["harmful","gentle","helpful","unknown"],"k":"A","sk":"Uses analogies to understand word relationships","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":42,"t":"mc","s":"The root **phil** means “love.” A **bibliophile** is a person who —","o":["sells books","repairs books","writes books","loves books"],"k":"D","sk":"Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":43,"t":"mc","s":"“Less is more” is an example of —","o":["onomatopoeia","a paradox","hyperbole","a simile"],"k":"B","sk":"Interprets paradox in context","b":"231–240","g":"VO"}]}]},{"id":"C","note":"Full practice test · 43 questions · harder","sections":[{"passage":{"title":"Recycling Rates in One City","genre":"chart","paras":["**Percentage of each material recycled in one city, 2020 and 2025**","Paper: 2020 — 30% · 2025 — 45% | Plastic: 2020 — 8% · 2025 — 12% | Glass: 2020 — 20% · 2025 — 18% | Metal cans: 2020 — 40% · 2025 — 55%","In 2023, the city introduced separate collection bins for paper and metal."]},"items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Which material had the LOWEST recycling rate in both years?","o":["metal cans","plastic","paper","glass"],"k":"B","sk":"Interprets information in charts or graphs","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"Which material’s recycling rate decreased between 2020 and 2025?","o":["paper","metal cans","plastic","glass"],"k":"D","sk":"Interprets information in charts or graphs","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"By how many percentage points did the paper recycling rate increase?","o":["15","30","45","50"],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes quantitative information in informational text","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":4,"t":"mc","s":"Which explanation is BEST supported by the information?","o":["The new bins were removed before 2025.","The new bins made glass recycling impossible.","The new bins may have helped increase paper and metal recycling.","The new bins caused plastic recycling in the city to triple in five years."],"k":"C","sk":"Integrates information from graphics and text","b":"231–240","g":"IT"},{"n":5,"t":"mc","s":"Why can we NOT be certain that the bins caused the increase?","o":["The data in the chart covers only one recycling material.","Other factors may also have changed between 2020 and 2025.","The figures show that recycling fell overall.","The bins were introduced after 2025."],"k":"B","sk":"Evaluates reasoning about cause and correlation","b":"231–240","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"The Milkmaids and the Smallpox Vaccine","genre":"informational","paras":["For centuries, smallpox was one of the most feared diseases in the world. It killed a large share of those it infected and left many survivors scarred or blind.","In the eighteenth century, people in parts of England noticed that milkmaids who had caught cowpox, a mild disease from cows, seemed not to catch smallpox. In 1796, the doctor Edward Jenner tested this idea. He took material from a cowpox sore and introduced it into a scratch on the arm of a boy named James Phipps. Weeks later, he exposed the boy to smallpox. The boy did not become ill.","Jenner called his method **vaccination**, from the Latin *vacca*, meaning cow. It spread rapidly across the world. Jenner was not the first to protect people against smallpox; an older practice, which used material from smallpox itself, had long been used in parts of Asia, Africa and the Middle East. But it carried a real risk of causing the disease. Vaccination was much safer.","Nearly two centuries later, after a global vaccination campaign led by the World Health Organization, smallpox was declared eradicated in 1980 — the first human disease to be wiped out completely. Today, Jenner’s experiment raises difficult questions: testing a dangerous idea on a child would not be considered ethical now."]},"items":[{"n":6,"t":"mc","s":"What observation led to Jenner’s experiment?","o":["Cows that ate certain plants in the fields never caught smallpox.","Children recovered from smallpox faster than adults.","Milkmaids who had caught cowpox seemed not to catch smallpox.","Doctors who treated smallpox rarely became ill."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines the cause of a situation","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":7,"t":"mc","s":"Where does the word **vaccination** come from, according to the text?","o":["the name of a disease in milkmaids","the English word for scratch","the name of the boy Jenner tested","the Latin word for cow"],"k":"D","sk":"Locates details in informational text","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":8,"t":"mc","s":"How is the text mainly organized?","o":["chronologically, from the problem through the discovery to its long-term results","as a comparison of the symptoms of smallpox and cowpox in people and in farm animals","as a set of instructions for giving vaccines","as an argument for and against vaccination"],"k":"A","sk":"Identifies chronology as a structure","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":9,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the author mention the older practice used in Asia, Africa and the Middle East?","o":["to prove that Jenner secretly copied his idea from other doctors of his time","to give a fuller history and show why vaccination was an improvement","to argue that the older practice was safer than vaccination","to explain where smallpox first appeared in the world"],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes how specific details contribute to meaning","b":"231–240","g":"IT"},{"n":10,"t":"mc","s":"What is the purpose of the final sentence?","o":["to suggest that smallpox may return in the future","to explain how vaccines are tested today","to show that the story raises ethical questions as well as celebrating success","to argue that Jenner should never have been honoured for his work on vaccination"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes the purpose of an ending","b":"231–240","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"Why the Dead Sea Is Shrinking","genre":"informational","paras":["The Dead Sea, which lies between Jordan to the east and Israel and the West Bank to the west, is the lowest point on land on Earth, more than 400 metres below sea level. Its water is so salty that swimmers float easily on the surface. But the lake is in trouble: its level has been falling by about a metre a year.","The main reason is that much less water now reaches it. For thousands of years, the Jordan River fed the Dead Sea. In the twentieth century, countries in the region began diverting much of the river’s water for farming and drinking. At the same time, the Dead Sea loses water through evaporation in the intense heat, and mineral companies pump its water into evaporation ponds to extract potash and other minerals.","The effects can be seen along the shore. As the water retreats, it leaves behind dry land in which thousands of **sinkholes** have formed: sudden collapses in the ground, some large enough to swallow cars or buildings.","Various solutions have been proposed, including a canal or pipeline to bring water from the Red Sea, but they raise questions about cost, environmental risk and cooperation between countries. For now, the Dead Sea continues to shrink."]},"items":[{"n":11,"t":"mc","s":"What is the central idea of the text?","o":["Swimmers float easily in the Dead Sea because of its salt.","The Dead Sea is the saltiest, lowest and most beautiful lake anywhere on Earth today.","A canal from the Red Sea has saved the Dead Sea from drying.","The Dead Sea is shrinking mainly because human activity has reduced its water supply."],"k":"D","sk":"Determines main/central idea in informational text","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":12,"t":"mc","s":"How does the third paragraph contribute to the text?","o":["It describes a dangerous visible effect of the shrinking lake.","It proposes a solution to the falling water level.","It explains how the lake was formed many millions of years ago.","It describes the minerals extracted from the lake."],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes how specific paragraphs contribute to meaning","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":13,"t":"mc","s":"What does the final sentence suggest?","o":["The lake has started to recover.","The canal will be finished soon.","The sinkholes have stopped forming.","The problem remains unsolved."],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes the purpose of an ending","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":14,"t":"ms","s":"According to the text, which TWO human activities contribute to the falling water level? Choose TWO answers.","o":["adding salt to the water for swimmers","diverting the Jordan River’s water for farming and drinking","pumping lake water into evaporation ponds to extract minerals","building hotels along the shore of the lake","digging canals that drain the lake into the sea"],"k":"B, C","sk":"Determines the cause of a situation","b":"231–240","g":"IT"},{"n":15,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the author describe the proposed solutions as raising “questions”?","o":["to suggest that the author supports the canal","to prove that the proposed solutions have already failed completely","to show that the solutions are complicated and uncertain","to explain how the canal would be built"],"k":"C","sk":"Determines author’s attitude in informational text","b":"231–240","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"The Long Silence","genre":"story","paras":["When Khalid’s older brother, Fawaz, came home after six years of study in Canada, the whole family went to the airport. Khalid had been ten when Fawaz left. Now he was sixteen, taller than his brother, and suddenly unsure what to say.","At home, everyone talked at once: aunts asking about the snow, cousins asking about the food, their mother asking whether he had eaten properly. Fawaz answered everyone, smiling. Khalid sat at the edge of the room and said nothing.","That night, Khalid found his brother on the roof, looking at the city lights. “You didn’t say anything all evening,” Fawaz said without turning round.","“Everyone else did,” Khalid replied. Then, because the silence felt too large: “You missed everything. My first match. When I broke my arm. When Grandfather—” He stopped.","Fawaz was quiet for a long time. “I know,” he said at last. “I used to call and ask Mother to describe your matches. Every goal. I wrote them down.” He took a worn notebook from his pocket and held it out. On the first page, in his brother’s careful handwriting: *Khalid’s first match. Two assists.*","Khalid turned the pages slowly. Six years of small things, recorded from the other side of the world."]},"items":[{"n":16,"t":"mc","s":"Why is Khalid “suddenly unsure what to say” at the airport?","o":["He is embarrassed that he is now taller than Fawaz.","Six years of separation have made his brother feel almost like a stranger.","He is angry that his brother returned home from Canada without any warning.","He has forgotten how to speak with his family."],"k":"B","sk":"Explains character motivation","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":17,"t":"mc","s":"What does Khalid’s unfinished sentence “When Grandfather—” suggest?","o":["A painful family event happened while Fawaz was away.","Khalid wants Fawaz to call their grandfather.","Their grandfather is waiting downstairs.","Khalid has simply forgotten what he wanted to say to Fawaz."],"k":"A","sk":"Makes inferences from literary text","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":18,"t":"mc","s":"How does the notebook change Khalid’s understanding of his brother?","o":["It shows that Fawaz wants to become a sports reporter.","It shows that Fawaz has been writing a novel in Canada.","It shows that Fawaz followed Khalid’s life closely despite the distance.","It shows that Fawaz was far too busy in Canada to think about home very often."],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes how events shape characters","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":19,"t":"mc","s":"What contrast does the author draw between the evening and the roof scene?","o":["the family’s anger and the brothers’ happiness","the noisy, crowded welcome and the quiet, honest conversation","the cold Canadian weather and the warm, humid Saudi summer night","the city lights and the darkness of the airport"],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes how structure contributes to meaning","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":20,"t":"mc","s":"Which theme does the story develop?","o":["Love can endure distance in ways that are not always visible.","Football is the most important part of family life.","Younger brothers should forgive older brothers immediately.","Studying abroad always damages family relationships."],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes the development of theme in literary text","b":"231–240","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"The Two Merchants of Basra","genre":"folk tale","paras":["Two merchants of Basra, Jamil and Rashid, each loaded a ship with dates and sailed for India. Halfway across the sea, a storm struck. Jamil’s crew threw half his cargo overboard to keep the ship afloat. Rashid refused. “Every sack is money,” he said. His ship, too heavy, took on water and sank, and Rashid was rescued with nothing.","In India, Jamil sold what remained of his dates at a good price, for few ships had survived the storm. On his return, he found Rashid begging in the harbour of Basra.","Jamil gave him a sack of silver. “Take it,” he said. “Start again.”","“Why?” asked Rashid bitterly. “You were lucky. I was not.”","“It was not luck,” said Jamil. “I lost half to save the rest. You tried to save everything and lost it all. I give you this not because you deserve it, but so that you may learn the lesson the sea tried to teach us both.”","Rashid took the silver. Years later, when he had become rich again, he was known throughout Basra as the merchant who always knew when to let go."]},"items":[{"n":21,"t":"mc","s":"How do the two merchants respond DIFFERENTLY to the storm?","o":["Jamil abandons his ship; Rashid stays to repair his.","Jamil turns back to Basra in the storm; Rashid sails on to India.","Jamil asks for help; Rashid refuses to accept help.","Jamil sacrifices part of his cargo; Rashid refuses to lose any."],"k":"D","sk":"Compares or contrasts characters","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":22,"t":"mc","s":"What is the moral of the tale?","o":["Sometimes accepting a smaller loss prevents a total one.","Wealth always comes from good luck.","Dates are more valuable in India than in Basra.","Merchants should never travel by sea, especially during the stormy season."],"k":"A","sk":"Determines the moral of a folk tale","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":23,"t":"mc","s":"What does Rashid’s claim “You were lucky” reveal about him at first?","o":["He believes that storms can be predicted.","He is grateful to Jamil for his generosity.","He refuses to see his own role in his loss.","He wants to become partners with Jamil."],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes dialogue to understand characters","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":24,"t":"mc","s":"Why does Jamil say he gives the silver “not because you deserve it”?","o":["He expects Rashid to pay him back with interest.","He wants to show all the people of Basra how rich and powerful he has become.","He is angry with Rashid and wants to humiliate him.","He wants his help to be a chance to learn, not a reward for the mistake."],"k":"D","sk":"Explains character motivation","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":25,"t":"mc","s":"What does the phrase “the merchant who always knew when to let go” show?","o":["Rashid never sailed on the sea again.","Rashid eventually learned the lesson Jamil described.","Rashid stopped trading and became a sailor.","Rashid later gave all of his wealth to the poor of Basra."],"k":"B","sk":"Understands how characters are developed or changed","b":"231–240","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"The Mapmaker","genre":"poem","paras":["He drew the coast in careful ink,","Each bay, each rock, each reef and sand;","He named the wells where camels drink,","And where he had not set his hand","","He wrote across the white: *Unknown.*","No monsters, as the old maps had,","No lies to make the empty shown","As full — just honest, and not sad.","","I think of him when I pretend","To know what I have never learned;","And wish I’d leave, as he did, end","Of knowledge marked, and blank page earned."],"poem":true},"items":[{"n":26,"t":"mc","s":"What does the mapmaker do with places he has not visited?","o":["He invents names for imaginary wells.","He leaves them blank and marks them as unknown.","He copies them from older maps.","He fills them with colourful drawings of monsters."],"k":"B","sk":"Locates details in poetry","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":27,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the speaker contrast the mapmaker with “the old maps”?","o":["to show that he believed in sea monsters","to show that he was less skilled than older mapmakers","to show that he chose honesty over invention","to show that the old maps were more beautiful"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes how specific parts of a poem contribute to meaning","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":28,"t":"mc","s":"How does the third stanza shift the poem’s focus?","o":["from the mapmaker’s work to the speaker’s own habits","from the present to the distant past","from honesty to the beauty of maps","from the busy coast to the empty interior of the desert"],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes how the organization of a poem affects its meaning","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":29,"t":"mc","s":"What does “blank page earned” suggest?","o":["The speaker has run out of paper for his maps.","The speaker wants to stop learning new things.","Empty pages in a notebook are more valuable than pages that are full.","Honestly admitting what you do not know is a kind of achievement."],"k":"D","sk":"Determines the meaning of a nonliteral phrase","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":30,"t":"mc","s":"What is the theme of the poem?","o":["Intellectual honesty means admitting the limits of one’s knowledge.","Old maps were full of dangerous mistakes.","Maps are the most important and useful tools that any traveller can carry.","People should travel to every unknown place."],"k":"A","sk":"Determines theme in poetry","b":"231–240","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":null,"items":[{"n":31,"t":"mc","s":"Read the sentence. “The scientist’s claims were **unfounded**; she had no data to support them.” What does **unfounded** mean?","o":["carefully tested","widely accepted","recently published","not based on evidence"],"k":"D","sk":"Uses context to determine word meaning","b":"221–230","g":"VO"},{"n":32,"t":"mc","s":"Which word suggests the MOST extreme fear?","o":["anxious","uneasy","petrified","nervous"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes nuances in meaning among related words","b":"221–230","g":"VO"},{"n":33,"t":"mc","s":"The prefix **retro-** means “backward.” A **retrospective** view looks —","o":["forward to the future","back at the past","down at the ground","around the present"],"k":"B","sk":"Uses a defined root and/or affix to determine word meaning","b":"221–230","g":"VO"},{"n":34,"t":"mc","s":"Which word has the most POSITIVE connotation?","o":["juvenile","childish","immature","youthful"],"k":"D","sk":"Understands precise connotations of words","b":"221–230","g":"VO"},{"n":35,"t":"mc","s":"The root **log** means “word” or “study.” **Etymology** is the study of —","o":["ancient buildings","the habits of insects","the origins of words","weather patterns"],"k":"C","sk":"Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":36,"t":"mc","s":"In which sentence does **appreciate** mean “to increase in value”?","o":["She learned to appreciate classical poetry.","The land is expected to appreciate over the next decade.","We appreciate the beauty of the mountains.","I really appreciate all of your help with my science project."],"k":"B","sk":"Determines which meaning of a multiple-meaning word fits","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":37,"t":"mc","s":"“The new manager proved to be a **Trojan horse**, secretly working for a rival company.” What does the allusion suggest?","o":["Someone who seemed trustworthy was hiding a harmful purpose.","Someone who came from an ancient city.","Someone who was very strong and fast at his job in the city.","Someone who loved horses and riding."],"k":"A","sk":"Interprets allusion in context","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":38,"t":"mc","s":"Which word BEST completes the sentence? “The theory was elegant; ___, it failed to explain the new observations.”","o":["similarly","thus","consequently","nonetheless"],"k":"D","sk":"Identifies words in context that show a contrast","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":39,"t":"mc","s":"Read the paragraph. “The novelist’s style was **verbose**. A single afternoon could fill twenty pages, and every sentence seemed to contain three adjectives where one would do.” What does **verbose** mean?","o":["clear, short and very concise","witty and amusing","using more words than needed","sad and gloomy"],"k":"C","sk":"Uses paragraph-level context to determine word meaning","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":40,"t":"mc","s":"“The silence was so loud that I could hear my own thoughts.” Which device combines opposites here?","o":["simile","paradox","understatement","alliteration"],"k":"B","sk":"Interprets paradox in context","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":41,"t":"mc","s":"Which analogy is CORRECT? **Chronic** is to **long-lasting** as **acute** is to —","o":["sudden and severe","old and familiar","mild and slow","hidden and secret"],"k":"A","sk":"Uses analogies to understand word relationships","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":42,"t":"mc","s":"The root **path** means “feeling.” **Telepathy** would be the ability to —","o":["speak many different foreign languages fluently and quickly","sense another person’s thoughts or feelings from a distance","see objects that are far away","heal people by touching them"],"k":"B","sk":"Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":43,"t":"mc","s":"“He’s not the brightest star in the sky,” said the coach about his worst player. What device is the coach using?","o":["understatement","hyperbole","onomatopoeia","alliteration"],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes figurative language: understatement","b":"231–240","g":"VO"}]}]},{"id":"D","note":"Full practice test · 43 questions · harder","sections":[{"passage":{"title":"How Habits Work","genre":"informational","paras":["Much of what we do each day happens without conscious decision. We brush our teeth, check our phones and take the same route to school almost automatically. Researchers who study behaviour estimate that a large share of daily actions, by some estimates around 40 per cent, are performed out of habit rather than deliberate choice.","Psychologists often describe a habit as a loop with three parts. First comes a **cue**, a signal such as a time of day, a place or a feeling. Then comes the **routine**, the behaviour itself. Finally comes the **reward**, some benefit that makes the brain more likely to repeat the loop next time.","Over time, as the loop is repeated, the behaviour shifts from areas of the brain associated with careful decision-making to areas associated with automatic action. This saves mental effort, which is useful. But it also explains why bad habits are so hard to break: they no longer feel like choices at all.","Research suggests that the most effective way to change a habit is not simply to rely on willpower. Instead, people can change the cue (for example, keeping the phone in another room), or keep the cue and reward but replace the routine with a better one."]},"items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"What is the central idea of the text?","o":["Willpower is the only reliable way to break a bad habit.","Habits follow a cue–routine–reward loop, which explains how they form and how they can be changed.","Checking phones is the most common habit in the world.","Most people make careful and deliberate decisions about almost everything they do during each day."],"k":"B","sk":"Determines main/central idea in informational text","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"In the habit loop, what is the role of the reward?","o":["It signals that the behaviour should begin.","It is the behaviour itself, such as brushing your teeth.","It makes the brain more likely to repeat the behaviour.","It stops the habit from forming at all."],"k":"C","sk":"Understands explicit relationships between ideas","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"Which example from the text shows changing the cue?","o":["keeping the phone in another room","brushing teeth every morning","taking the same route to school","relying on willpower alone"],"k":"A","sk":"Identifies evidence that supports a statement","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":4,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the author include the phrase “by some estimates”?","o":["to prove that the figure is exactly correct","to suggest that habits are not important","to show that the author strongly disagrees with the research","to signal that the figure is approximate and not certain"],"k":"D","sk":"Evaluates the certainty of claims","b":"231–240","g":"IT"},{"n":5,"t":"mc","s":"According to the third paragraph, why are bad habits hard to break?","o":["They give much larger rewards than good habits.","Once automatic, they no longer feel like choices.","They are formed in a single day and never change.","They are controlled by a different person’s brain."],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes implicit relationships between ideas","b":"231–240","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"The Last Rehearsal","genre":"drama","paras":["*(A school stage, the night before a performance. The director, MR. HAITHAM, sits in the empty seats. OSAMA, 16, finishes a speech in which his character learns that his father has died.)*","MR. HAITHAM: Stop. *(He stands.)* Osama, what was that?","OSAMA: *(confused)* The speech. I remembered every word.","MR. HAITHAM: You did. And you cried exactly on the line where I marked “cry” in the script. That is the problem.","OSAMA: You told me to cry there.","MR. HAITHAM: *(walking toward the stage)* I told you where the character breaks. I did not tell you to perform breaking. *(He pauses.)* When your grandfather died last year, did you cry on cue?","*(Long silence. OSAMA looks down.)*","OSAMA: *(quietly)* No. I didn’t cry at all. Not for days. I just… kept doing ordinary things. Tying my shoes. Answering the phone.","MR. HAITHAM: *(gently)* Then do that. Do ordinary things. Let the audience break for you.","*(OSAMA begins the speech again — slowly, without tears, folding a shirt as he speaks. MR. HAITHAM sits down without a word.)*"]},"items":[{"n":6,"t":"mc","s":"What problem does Mr. Haitham see in Osama’s first performance?","o":["Osama has changed the words of the script.","Osama has forgotten many of his lines.","Osama is speaking far too quietly to be heard at the back.","It is technically correct but emotionally false."],"k":"D","sk":"Identifies conflict in literary text","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":7,"t":"mc","s":"What does Mr. Haitham’s silence at the end suggest?","o":["He has fallen asleep in the empty seats.","He is angry that Osama ignored his advice.","He is moved and satisfied by the new performance.","He is secretly planning to replace Osama in the play."],"k":"C","sk":"Makes inferences from literary text","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":8,"t":"mc","s":"What distinction does Mr. Haitham make between “where the character breaks” and “perform breaking”?","o":["Real emotion should grow from the moment, not be displayed on command.","The script should not include instructions for actors.","Actors should never show any emotion at all while they are on the stage.","Crying is only allowed at the end of a play."],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes dialogue to understand theme","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":9,"t":"mc","s":"Why does Mr. Haitham ask about Osama’s grandfather?","o":["to find out whether Osama is too sad to perform","to criticise Osama for not crying at the funeral","to help Osama draw on his own honest experience of grief","to change the subject quickly because the rehearsal is now over"],"k":"C","sk":"Explains character motivation","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":10,"t":"mc","s":"What is the effect of the stage direction showing Osama folding a shirt?","o":["It shows grief through an ordinary action, making it more believable.","It shows that the costume needs to be changed.","It shows that Osama has forgotten the speech.","It shows that Osama is bored with rehearsing and just wants to go home."],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes use of stage directions","b":"231–240","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"Projects Should Replace Final Exams","genre":"argument","paras":["At the end of every term, students cram facts into their memories, write them down in a two-hour exam and forget most of them within weeks. Schools should replace most final exams with extended projects.","Projects measure deeper skills. A two-hour exam rewards students who memorise quickly and perform well under pressure. A project lasting several weeks requires research, planning, problem-solving and the ability to improve work after feedback — the skills universities and employers say they value most.","Projects also reduce harmful stress. Many students report severe anxiety before exams, and a single bad morning can undo a term’s work. A project allows students to show their learning over time.","Critics argue that projects are easier to cheat on, because students can copy from the internet or get help from family. This is a serious concern. However, teachers can require drafts, notes and short oral presentations in which students explain and defend their work. It is difficult to defend work you did not do.","Exams test what students can remember on one day. Projects reveal what they can do. Schools should decide which of these matters more."]},"items":[{"n":11,"t":"mc","s":"What is the author’s claim?","o":["Schools should allow students to choose all of their own subjects.","Schools should make final exams longer and more difficult.","Schools should stop giving students any feedback.","Schools should replace most final exams with extended projects."],"k":"D","sk":"Determines author’s main argument or claim","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":12,"t":"mc","s":"Which reason does the author support by referring to universities and employers?","o":["Projects reduce harmful stress before exams.","Projects measure deeper, more valuable skills.","Projects are harder to cheat on than exams.","Projects take less time to mark than exams."],"k":"B","sk":"Identifies reasons that support a claim","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":13,"t":"mc","s":"How does the author respond to the concern about cheating?","o":["by denying that students ever cheat on projects or receive help from others","by agreeing that exams are therefore better","by acknowledging it and proposing drafts, notes and oral defences","by ignoring the concern and changing the subject"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes how an author acknowledges and responds to conflicting views","b":"231–240","g":"IT"},{"n":14,"t":"mc","s":"What is the rhetorical effect of the final paragraph’s contrast between “remember” and “do”?","o":["It introduces a new counterclaim about cheating.","It frames the choice so that projects seem more meaningful.","It suggests that exams and projects are the same.","It shows that the author values memory above all skills."],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes rhetorical devices: parallelism and contrast","b":"231–240","g":"IT"},{"n":15,"t":"mc","s":"Which claim in the text needs MORE evidence to be convincing?","o":["Students forget most of what they memorise within weeks.","Teachers can require drafts and notes.","Some critics are concerned about cheating.","A project lasting several weeks requires careful planning."],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes inadequately supported claims","b":"231–240","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"Mobile Data Plans","genre":"chart","paras":["**Monthly mobile plans**","Plan Basic: 10 GB data · 45 riyals per month · Plan Plus: 30 GB data · 85 riyals per month · Plan Unlimited: unlimited data · 150 riyals per month","Extra data on Basic or Plus: 5 riyals per additional GB. Students with a valid ID receive 20% off Plan Plus."]},"items":[{"n":16,"t":"mc","s":"What does a student with a valid ID pay for Plan Plus each month?","o":["85 riyals","17 riyals","65 riyals","68 riyals"],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes quantitative information in informational text","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":17,"t":"mc","s":"Which customer would MOST benefit from Plan Unlimited?","o":["someone who regularly uses far more than 30 GB a month","a student who uses only about 5 GB of data in a month","someone who mainly makes phone calls","a person who uses 12 GB in most months"],"k":"A","sk":"Integrates information from graphics and text","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":18,"t":"mc","s":"A person on Plan Basic uses 20 GB in a month. What is the total cost?","o":["50 riyals","85 riyals","145 riyals","95 riyals"],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes quantitative information in informational text","b":"231–240","g":"IT"},{"n":19,"t":"mc","s":"For a non-student who uses 25 GB a month, which plan is cheapest?","o":["Plan Unlimited","Plan Basic","Plan Plus","Plan Basic and Plan Plus cost the same."],"k":"C","sk":"Integrates information from a text feature","b":"231–240","g":"IT"},{"n":20,"t":"mc","s":"At how many GB per month does Plan Basic, with extra data, begin to cost MORE than Plan Plus?","o":["more than 10 GB","more than 18 GB","more than 30 GB","more than 25 GB"],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes quantitative information in informational text","b":"231–240","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"The Best Player on the Team","genre":"story","paras":["I want to be clear: our team lost the semi-final because of the others, not me. I scored our only goal, after all. Everyone saw it.","Coach Nabil says football is a team game. He says it so often that I stopped listening months ago. In the first half, Majid kept shouting “Pass!” at me, but Majid has never scored more than two goals in a season, so I am not sure why I should have listened to him. I took my chance from a difficult angle. The goalkeeper saved it. These things happen.","In the second half, I scored. A beautiful shot, if I say so myself. After that, I suppose I stopped running back to defend, but that is what defenders are for. When they scored their two goals, both down my side of the pitch, the defenders looked at me as if it were my fault.","Afterwards, nobody spoke to me in the changing room. Coach Nabil said, quietly, “Fourteen shots. One goal. Zero passes.” I think he meant it as a compliment to my shooting. Then he said that I would start the next match on the bench, which shows how little he understands the game."]},"items":[{"n":21,"t":"mc","s":"What does the silence in the changing room suggest?","o":["His teammates are waiting for the coach.","His teammates admire his goal too much to speak.","His teammates are too tired to speak.","His teammates blame him for the loss."],"k":"D","sk":"Makes inferences from literary text","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":22,"t":"mc","s":"What kind of narrator tells this story?","o":["an unreliable first-person narrator whose view the reader should question","a reliable third-person narrator who knows every single character’s thoughts","a narrator who is one of the defenders on the team","an objective narrator who reports events without opinions"],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes the effect of narrator’s point of view","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":23,"t":"mc","s":"Which detail BEST reveals that the narrator contributed to the defeat?","o":["The goalkeeper saved his shot from a difficult angle.","Both of the other team’s goals came down his side after he stopped defending.","He scored the team’s only goal of the whole semi-final with a beautiful shot.","Majid has never scored more than two goals in a season."],"k":"B","sk":"Determines details that support an inference","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":24,"t":"mc","s":"What is ironic about the narrator’s reaction to “Fourteen shots. One goal. Zero passes.”?","o":["He is angry because he believes he made many passes.","He thinks that the coach is actually talking about another player on the team.","He takes as praise what is clearly a criticism of his selfishness.","He realises at once that he played badly."],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes dramatic irony","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":25,"t":"mc","s":"What theme does the author develop through the narrator’s blindness to his own faults?","o":["Semi-finals are harder to win than finals.","Coaches rarely understand the players on their teams.","Talented players should always be allowed to shoot.","Pride can prevent people from seeing their own mistakes."],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes the development of theme in literary text","b":"231–240","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"Glass","genre":"poem","paras":["My mother keeps one glass apart,","A plain one, chipped along the rim;","She will not let us use it. Art,","She says, is not for every whim.","","It was her mother’s, once, and then","It crossed two borders in her hand,","Wrapped up in cloth, and once again","In paper, in a different land.","","It is not beautiful. It’s small.","It cannot hold a pound of rice.","But it holds everything — the hall,","The voices, and the price."],"poem":true},"items":[{"n":26,"t":"mc","s":"What does the second stanza reveal about the glass’s history?","o":["It was a gift from the poet’s teacher.","It was bought cheaply in a busy market in a different land many years ago.","It was carried carefully through the family’s journeys to new countries.","It was broken and repaired several times."],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes how specific parts of a poem contribute to meaning","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":27,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the mother call the plain glass “Art”?","o":["It could be sold for a great deal of money.","It has a value to her far beyond its appearance or use.","It was made long ago by a famous and very skilled glass artist.","It is decorated with beautiful paintings."],"k":"B","sk":"Determines the meaning of a nonliteral phrase","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":28,"t":"mc","s":"What is the effect of the short sentences “It is not beautiful. It’s small.”?","o":["They state plainly how ordinary the glass is, preparing for a contrast.","They show that the speaker secretly dislikes the glass and wants it thrown away.","They describe the size of the family’s kitchen.","They show that the poem is about to end."],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes how sentence structure contributes to meaning","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":29,"t":"mc","s":"In the final lines, what does the glass “hold”?","o":["the family’s memories, history and sacrifices","enough rice and bread to feed the whole family","the family’s money and valuables","water for the mother’s plants"],"k":"A","sk":"Interprets symbolism in literary text","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":30,"t":"mc","s":"What is the theme of the poem?","o":["Mothers should share everything with their children.","Old glasses in a family should always be replaced with new ones.","Travelling to new countries is always difficult.","Ordinary objects can carry a family’s deepest history and meaning."],"k":"D","sk":"Determines theme in poetry","b":"231–240","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":null,"items":[{"n":31,"t":"mc","s":"Which word BEST completes the sentence? “The first experiment failed; ___, the team learned enough to design a better one.”","o":["for instance","nevertheless","likewise","therefore"],"k":"B","sk":"Identifies words in context that show a contrast","b":"221–230","g":"VO"},{"n":32,"t":"mc","s":"The prefix **mono-** means “one.” A **monopoly** exists when —","o":["two companies share a market","no company sells a product","one company controls a market","many companies compete equally"],"k":"C","sk":"Uses a defined root and/or affix to determine word meaning","b":"221–230","g":"VO"},{"n":33,"t":"mc","s":"Which word has the most NEGATIVE connotation?","o":["obstinate","resolute","determined","firm"],"k":"A","sk":"Understands precise connotations of words","b":"221–230","g":"VO"},{"n":34,"t":"mc","s":"“He gave a **Herculean** effort to finish the marathon.” What does the allusion suggest about the effort?","o":["It was quick and careless.","It was easy, relaxed and done without any real effort.","It required enormous strength and determination.","It was dishonest and unfair."],"k":"C","sk":"Interprets allusion in context","b":"221–230","g":"VO"},{"n":35,"t":"mc","s":"The root **voc** means “call” or “voice.” An **equivocal** answer is one that —","o":["is shouted loudly so that everyone hears","is completely honest","is given very quickly","can be understood in more than one way"],"k":"D","sk":"Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":36,"t":"mc","s":"Read the sentence. “The professor’s **erudite** lecture was full of references to history, science and literature.” What does **erudite** mean?","o":["very short and simple","showing great learning","loud and dramatic","boring and repetitive"],"k":"B","sk":"Uses context to determine word meaning","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":37,"t":"mc","s":"Which word suggests the MOST complete destruction?","o":["undermine","annihilate","harm","weaken"],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes nuances in meaning among related words","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":38,"t":"mc","s":"In which sentence does **temper** mean “to soften or moderate”?","o":["She tempered her criticism with words of praise.","Their tempers rose as the argument continued.","He lost his temper during the meeting.","The child has a quick temper."],"k":"A","sk":"Determines which meaning of a multiple-meaning word fits","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":39,"t":"mc","s":"“After the scandal, the minister became a **pariah** in political circles.” What does **pariah** mean?","o":["a person who leads a group","a person who keeps secrets","a person who is admired by others","a person who is rejected by others"],"k":"D","sk":"Uses context to determine word meaning","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":40,"t":"mc","s":"Read the paragraph. “The diplomat’s tone was **conciliatory**. She praised both sides, admitted her own country’s mistakes and suggested a meeting to find common ground.” What does **conciliatory** mean?","o":["secret and suspicious","meant to start a quarrel","intended to make peace","cold and unfriendly"],"k":"C","sk":"Uses paragraph-level context to determine word meaning","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":41,"t":"mc","s":"“The old library was a cemetery of forgotten ideas.” What does this metaphor suggest?","o":["The library was very quiet and calm.","The library had been built right next to an old city cemetery.","The ideas in the books had been neglected and left unused.","The books were about history and death."],"k":"C","sk":"Interprets metaphor in context","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":42,"t":"mc","s":"Which analogy is CORRECT? **Prologue** is to **book** as **overture** is to —","o":["novel","opera","painting","statue"],"k":"B","sk":"Uses analogies to understand word relationships","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":43,"t":"mc","s":"“I can resist everything except temptation.” What makes this sentence a paradox?","o":["It exaggerates for effect.","It compares two different things directly by using the words “like” or “as”.","It repeats the same sound at the start of words.","It seems to contradict itself but makes a humorous point about weakness."],"k":"D","sk":"Interprets paradox in context","b":"231–240","g":"VO"}]}]},{"id":"E","note":"Final full practice test · 43 questions · hardest","sections":[{"passage":{"title":"The Stone That Unlocked a Language","genre":"informational","paras":["For more than a thousand years, no one could read Egyptian hieroglyphs. Travellers admired the elegant symbols on temple walls, but their meaning had been lost.","In 1799, French soldiers rebuilding a fort near the town of Rashid, known in Europe as Rosetta, found a broken slab of dark stone covered in writing. Scholars quickly realised its importance: it carried the same official decree in three scripts — Egyptian hieroglyphs, a later Egyptian script called Demotic, and ancient Greek. Because scholars could read Greek, the stone offered a key to the other two.","Unlocking that key took more than twenty years. Several scholars made progress, but in 1822 the French scholar Jean-François Champollion announced the breakthrough. He showed that hieroglyphs were not simply pictures of ideas, as many had believed, but combined symbols for sounds with symbols for meanings. His knowledge of Coptic, a later form of the Egyptian language, was crucial.","The decipherment opened thousands of years of Egyptian history to modern readers. Today the stone is displayed in the British Museum in London, and its name has become a common phrase for anything that unlocks a difficult problem."]},"items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"What does the final sentence explain about the phrase “Rosetta Stone”?","o":["It is now used for any ancient stone that is carved with writing in three different scripts.","It is now used figuratively for anything that unlocks a difficult problem.","It is used only by scholars of ancient history.","It refers to a type of stone found in temples."],"k":"B","sk":"Determines the meaning of words and phrases as used in a text","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"How is the text mainly organized?","o":["chronologically, from the lost language to its decipherment and legacy","as an argument about where the stone should be kept","as a comparison of ancient Greek and Egyptian writing systems and their uses","as a description of the stone’s physical features"],"k":"A","sk":"Identifies chronology as a structure","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"Why was the Rosetta Stone so valuable to scholars?","o":["It was the oldest and largest stone ever found anywhere in Egypt by soldiers.","It explained how the pyramids were built.","It contained a dictionary of hieroglyphic symbols.","It carried the same text in a known language and in two unknown scripts."],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes implicit relationships between ideas","b":"231–240","g":"IT"},{"n":4,"t":"mc","s":"What misunderstanding did Champollion correct?","o":["that the stone had been found by soldiers","that ancient Greek could not be read","that hieroglyphs were only pictures of ideas","that Coptic was unrelated to Egyptian"],"k":"C","sk":"Locates details in informational text","b":"231–240","g":"IT"},{"n":5,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the author mention that “Several scholars made progress”?","o":["to explain why the stone is in London","to acknowledge that the breakthrough built on others’ work","to show that the stone was difficult to transport","to argue that Champollion did not really deserve the credit he was given"],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes how specific details contribute to meaning","b":"231–240","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"The Lamp in the Window","genre":"story","paras":["For as long as anyone in the village could remember, old Umm Saleh had kept a lamp burning in her window every night. Children said she was waiting for a ghost. Adults said she was eccentric and wasted oil. Nobody asked her.","Hamdan asked her the year he turned fifteen, when his family sent him each evening to deliver bread to the widows at the edge of the village. He found her trimming the wick.","“My husband went to sea in the winter of the great storm,” she said, “before you were born. His boat was found. He was not.” She set the glass back over the flame. “The first year, I lit it because I hoped. The tenth year, because I could not stop. Now…” She looked out at the dark road. “Now I light it for whoever is coming home late and does not know the way.”","That spring, Hamdan’s cousin, returning from the city on foot after his car broke down in the wadi, followed a single light across three kilometres of dark hills to the village. He arrived at Umm Saleh’s door, exhausted, at two in the morning.","When Umm Saleh died two years later, the village held a meeting about her house. At the end of it, without anyone quite deciding, Hamdan found himself carrying a can of oil up her steps at dusk."]},"items":[{"n":6,"t":"mc","s":"How do the villagers’ explanations of the lamp reflect their attitude toward Umm Saleh?","o":["They are afraid that she will harm their children.","They respect her greatly and protect her secret carefully from strangers.","They help her buy oil for the lamp each week.","They invent stories about her instead of trying to understand her."],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes how characters perceive one another","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":7,"t":"mc","s":"How does the meaning of the lamp change for Umm Saleh over the years?","o":["from hope, to habit, to a gift for others","from fear, to anger, to final forgiveness","from duty, to pride, to regret","from grief, to joy, to indifference"],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes how a symbol develops over a text","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":8,"t":"mc","s":"How does the cousin’s journey develop the story’s central idea?","o":["It shows that the cousin was afraid of the dark.","It shows that cars should not be driven through wadis.","It shows that Umm Saleh’s lamp truly guides people home.","It shows that Hamdan’s family lives far from the village."],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes how events develop theme","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":9,"t":"mc","s":"What does the phrase “without anyone quite deciding” suggest about Hamdan’s action?","o":["He plans to sell Umm Saleh’s house to strangers.","He has misunderstood what the meeting decided.","He was ordered by the village meeting to go and light the lamp at dusk every evening.","He continues the tradition naturally, out of understanding rather than instruction."],"k":"D","sk":"Makes inferences from literary text","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":10,"t":"mc","s":"Which theme does the story develop?","o":["Villagers should not tell strange stories about their older neighbours.","Lamps are more reliable than modern electric lights.","Private grief can be transformed into lasting service to others.","Young people should deliver bread to widows each evening."],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes the development of theme in literary text","b":"231–240","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"The Debate","genre":"drama","paras":["*(A school auditorium. A debate final. FAISAL argues that technology has made people more connected; SULTAN argues that it has made them lonelier. A MODERATOR sits between them. Both boys have phones on the table.)*","FAISAL: My opponent says technology isolates us. Yet I can speak to my cousin in Canada every day. Last week, my class organised a charity drive entirely online and raised money in a single afternoon. Technology does not divide us. It brings us closer.","*(As FAISAL sits down, his phone buzzes. He glances at it and smiles, typing a reply as SULTAN stands.)*","SULTAN: My opponent makes good points. I, too, speak to relatives far away. But let me ask the audience a question. *(He pauses.)* How many of you have sat at a family dinner where everyone was looking at a screen? *(Hands rise across the hall.)* Connection is not only about reaching people far away. It is about being present with the people beside us.","*(FAISAL, still typing, does not look up. Some of the audience laughs.)*","MODERATOR: *(dryly)* Mr. Faisal, would you like to respond?","FAISAL: *(looking up, startled)* Sorry — could you repeat the question?"]},"items":[{"n":11,"t":"mc","s":"What is the central disagreement of the debate?","o":["whether students should be allowed to bring their phones into school","whether technology makes people more connected or more isolated","whether relatives abroad should visit more often","whether online charity drives raise enough money"],"k":"B","sk":"Identifies conflict in literary text","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":12,"t":"mc","s":"How do the stage directions create irony?","o":["Faisal’s behaviour on his phone undermines his own argument.","The audience refuses to raise their hands.","Sultan uses his phone to look up evidence during his speech.","The moderator leaves the stage before the debate ends."],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes dramatic irony","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":13,"t":"mc","s":"Why does Sultan begin by agreeing that he, too, speaks to relatives far away?","o":["to show the audience that he has secretly changed sides in the debate","to waste time because he has no argument","to prove that Faisal’s examples are false","to appear fair before shifting the meaning of “connection”"],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes how an author acknowledges and responds to conflicting views","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":14,"t":"mc","s":"What tone does the moderator’s line have, as shown by *(dryly)*?","o":["nervous and unsure","quietly sarcastic","warm and admiring","angry and shouting"],"k":"B","sk":"Determines tone in literary text","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":15,"t":"mc","s":"What does the scene suggest about the debate’s question?","o":["Debates should never be held about technology.","People who use phones always win debates.","Technology can connect people across distance while distracting them from those nearby.","Technology has no real effect at all on how people relate to each other at home or at work."],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes the development of theme in literary text","b":"231–240","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"The Lamp","genre":"poem","paras":["Knowledge, my teacher said, is not a sun","That floods the world and leaves no corner dark;","It is a lamp, a small and patient one,","That shows the next three steps, and not the park,","","Nor all the city, nor the distant sea —","Just stone, and stair, and where the path will bend.","And if you lift it higher, you will see","Not more, but less; the dark has no clear end.","","So carry it, he said, and carry low,","And do not curse the shadows at your side;","The ones who walk most surely do not know","The whole road — only that the lamp is wide","","Enough for one more step, and one, and one,","Until the walking is the journey done."],"poem":true},"items":[{"n":16,"t":"mc","s":"What is the tone of the poem?","o":["calm, wise and reassuring","playful and mocking","fearful and anxious","angry, bitter and frustrated"],"k":"A","sk":"Determines tone in poetry","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":17,"t":"mc","s":"What contrast does the teacher draw in the first stanza?","o":["between the bright light of the day and the complete darkness of the night","between knowledge as an all-revealing sun and knowledge as a small lamp","between the teacher’s knowledge and the student’s ignorance","between the city and the countryside"],"k":"B","sk":"Interprets extended metaphor","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":18,"t":"mc","s":"What does the paradox “if you lift it higher, you will see / Not more, but less” suggest?","o":["Trying to know everything at once can make understanding harder.","The teacher’s eyesight is becoming worse with age.","Darkness grows larger when the lamp is turned off.","Lamps should always be held as high as possible so that people can see."],"k":"A","sk":"Interprets paradox in context","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":19,"t":"mc","s":"What attitude toward uncertainty does the third stanza recommend?","o":["running quickly to escape the shadows","complaining about the darkness that surrounds us","accepting what is unknown while moving forward steadily","refusing to move at all until the whole road is clearly visible"],"k":"C","sk":"Determines theme in poetry","b":"231–240","g":"LT"},{"n":20,"t":"mc","s":"How do the final two lines, set apart as a couplet, affect the poem?","o":["They describe the teacher’s death at the end of the journey.","They introduce a new speaker who strongly disagrees with the teacher’s advice.","They change the setting from the road to the classroom.","They conclude the image with the idea that progress comes step by step."],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes how the organization or form of a poem affects its meaning","b":"231–240","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"Should Social Media Have a Minimum Age of 16?","genre":"viewpoints","paras":["**Viewpoint 1 — Dr. Amal, child psychologist:** I see the effects every week: teenagers comparing themselves with filtered images, losing sleep to endless scrolling, and being bullied in ways that follow them home. Some countries have begun introducing minimum ages of 16 for social media. Younger teenagers are still developing the judgement needed to manage these pressures. A higher age limit would give them time to grow before facing them.","**Viewpoint 2 — Yazeed, 17, student:** Social media is where my generation talks, learns and creates. I learned graphic design from online tutorials and found friends who share my interests. A ban would not remove the risks; it would push younger users to lie about their age or use less safe platforms. Instead of banning teenagers, we should teach digital skills in school and require platforms to design safer features."]},"items":[{"n":21,"t":"mc","s":"What is Dr. Amal’s main claim?","o":["A minimum age of 16 would protect younger teenagers from harmful pressures.","Social media should be completely banned for people of all ages in every country.","Teenagers should spend more time on social media.","Bullying happens only on social media."],"k":"A","sk":"Determines author’s main argument or claim","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":22,"t":"mc","s":"What is the main basis of Dr. Amal’s evidence?","o":["interviews with technology companies","national statistics on social media use","her professional observations of teenagers","the results of a school survey"],"k":"C","sk":"Evaluates evidence used to support claims","b":"231–240","g":"IT"},{"n":23,"t":"mc","s":"What unintended consequence of a ban does Yazeed predict?","o":["Schools would have to close their computer rooms.","Younger users would lie about their age or use less safe platforms.","Platforms would become more expensive for adults.","Teenagers would stop using the internet entirely, even for their homework."],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes reasoning in an argument","b":"231–240","g":"IT"},{"n":24,"t":"mc","s":"On which point would BOTH writers most likely agree?","o":["Social media has no real benefits at all for teenagers.","Platforms are already completely safe.","Schools should stop teaching technology.","Social media carries real risks for young people."],"k":"D","sk":"Compares and contrasts viewpoints in multiple texts","b":"231–240","g":"IT"},{"n":25,"t":"mc","s":"How does Yazeed’s solution differ from Dr. Amal’s?","o":["He favours even stricter age limits for social media than she does.","He wants platforms to be closed for good.","He wants parents to decide everything.","He favours education and safer design over age restrictions."],"k":"D","sk":"Compares and contrasts viewpoints in multiple texts","b":"231–240","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"How Your Phone Knows Where You Are","genre":"informational","paras":["When a map app shows your position as a small blue dot, it is relying on a network of satellites orbiting about 20,000 kilometres above the Earth. The best-known system is GPS, operated by the United States, but several others exist.","Each satellite carries an extremely precise atomic clock and continuously broadcasts its position and the exact time. A receiver in your phone picks up these signals and calculates how long each one took to arrive. Because radio signals travel at the speed of light, the delay reveals the distance to each satellite. With signals from at least four satellites, the receiver can work out its position in three dimensions and correct its own less precise clock — a process related to **trilateration**.","Precision matters enormously. An error of just one millionth of a second would place you about 300 metres from your real position. The system must even account for Einstein’s theories of relativity: because the satellites move fast and experience weaker gravity, their clocks run at a slightly different rate from clocks on the ground, by about 38 millionths of a second per day. Without correction, positions would drift by kilometres each day.","The same signals that guide drivers also help time financial transactions, coordinate power grids and track ships at sea, making satellite navigation one of the most important invisible technologies of modern life."]},"items":[{"n":26,"t":"mc","s":"How does a receiver calculate its distance from each satellite?","o":["by asking the satellite for the distance directly","by measuring how long the signal took to arrive","by comparing the colour of each signal","by measuring the strength of the signal"],"k":"B","sk":"Understands explicit relationships between ideas","b":"231–240","g":"IT"},{"n":27,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the receiver need signals from at least four satellites?","o":["because three satellites always give the wrong answer","because each satellite in the network covers only one country or region","to find its position in three dimensions and correct its own clock","to receive enough electricity to work properly"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes implicit relationships between ideas","b":"231–240","g":"IT"},{"n":28,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the author give the example of an error of one millionth of a second?","o":["to show how tiny timing errors lead to large position errors","to explain how fast phones can calculate","to compare GPS with other systems","to prove that satellites are often inaccurate and cannot be trusted"],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes how specific details contribute to meaning","b":"231–240","g":"IT"},{"n":29,"t":"mc","s":"What would happen without relativity corrections, according to the text?","o":["Satellites would fall out of orbit.","Phones would stop receiving any signals.","Atomic clocks would stop working completely.","Positions would drift by kilometres each day."],"k":"D","sk":"Determines the effect of a situation","b":"231–240","g":"IT"},{"n":30,"t":"mc","s":"What is the purpose of the final paragraph?","o":["to warn readers about relying too much on phones","to describe the history of the GPS network from its very beginnings","to show that satellite timing supports many systems beyond navigation","to explain how map apps display a blue dot"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes the purpose of an ending","b":"231–240","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":null,"items":[{"n":31,"t":"mc","s":"Which word has the most POSITIVE connotation?","o":["frugal","stingy","cheap","tight-fisted"],"k":"A","sk":"Understands precise connotations of words","b":"221–230","g":"VO"},{"n":32,"t":"mc","s":"The root **morph** means “shape.” Something **amorphous** —","o":["changes colour","has no clear shape","is very heavy","has a perfect shape"],"k":"B","sk":"Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":33,"t":"mc","s":"Read the paragraph. “The minister’s promise proved **illusory**. The new hospital was never built, the funding disappeared, and the site remained an empty field.” What does **illusory** mean?","o":["completed early","not real; based on false appearance","very expensive","carefully planned and well organised"],"k":"B","sk":"Uses paragraph-level context to determine word meaning","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":34,"t":"mc","s":"Which word suggests the MOST bitter and lasting anger?","o":["rancour","annoyance","irritation","displeasure"],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes nuances in meaning among related words","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":35,"t":"mc","s":"In which sentence does **qualify** mean “to limit or add conditions to a statement”?","o":["Only students with a valid ID qualify for the discount.","He hopes to qualify for the national team.","The course will qualify her to work as a nurse.","She qualified her praise by noting a few weaknesses."],"k":"D","sk":"Determines which meaning of a multiple-meaning word fits","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":36,"t":"mc","s":"“The company’s rapid expansion turned out to be its **Achilles’ heel**.” What does the allusion suggest?","o":["The expansion was its greatest strength.","The expansion was slow and careful.","The expansion became a fatal weakness.","The expansion was planned in Greece."],"k":"C","sk":"Interprets allusion in context","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":37,"t":"mc","s":"Which word BEST completes the sentence? “The evidence strongly suggests the theory is correct; ___, more research is needed before it can be accepted.”","o":["similarly","therefore","consequently","nonetheless"],"k":"D","sk":"Identifies words in context that show a contrast","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":38,"t":"mc","s":"The prefix **ante-** means “before.” What does **antebellum** describe?","o":["a type of ancient weapon","the period before a war","a building used for meetings","the period after a war"],"k":"B","sk":"Uses a defined root and/or affix to determine word meaning","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":39,"t":"mc","s":"“His words were a double-edged sword.” What does this metaphor suggest?","o":["His words could help and harm at the same time.","His words were sharp, cruel and much too loud.","He repeated everything twice.","He spoke about the history of weapons."],"k":"A","sk":"Interprets metaphor in context","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":40,"t":"mc","s":"Read the sentence. “The judge’s ruling was **unequivocal**: there was no doubt about what she meant.” What does **unequivocal** mean?","o":["temporary","deliberately vague","completely clear","unfair"],"k":"C","sk":"Uses context to determine word meaning","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":41,"t":"mc","s":"Which analogy is CORRECT? **Philanthropist** is to **generosity** as **misanthrope** is to —","o":["love of books","skill with money","fear of heights","dislike of people"],"k":"D","sk":"Uses analogies to understand word relationships","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":42,"t":"mc","s":"“It was a small step for the team, but a giant leap for the school.” This sentence uses —","o":["an idiom about walking","personification of the school and its building","contrast and parallel structure for emphasis","onomatopoeia to imitate a sound"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes rhetorical devices: parallelism and contrast","b":"231–240","g":"VO"},{"n":43,"t":"mc","s":"“After losing everything in the flood, he said, ‘It has been a slightly difficult week.’” What device does this sentence use, and why?","o":["understatement, to show restraint in the face of great loss","paradox, to show that the flood was good","simile, to compare the flood to a week","hyperbole, to exaggerate how bad the week really was for him"],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes figurative language: understatement","b":"231–240","g":"VO"}]}]}],"pass":[{"title":"The Architect’s Son","genre":"story","paras":["For most of Yasir’s childhood, his father had been a voice on the phone and a name on the side of buildings. As one of the country’s leading architects, he designed towers in Riyadh and hotels in Jeddah, and he was always on his way to somewhere else.","Yasir had learned not to ask when he would be home. At his school prize-giving, the seat beside his mother was empty again. Afterwards, his father called to apologise. Yasir said, “It’s fine,” in the flat voice he had perfected, and ended the call first.","In the summer Yasir turned fifteen, his father had a mild heart attack and was ordered to rest for three months. For the first time, he was home all day. Yasir avoided him. He did not know how to be angry at a man who now moved so slowly.","One afternoon, looking for a charger, Yasir opened the door of his father’s study and stopped. On the desk stood a scale model of a small, ordinary house with a courtyard and a single palm tree. It was meticulous: tiny windows, a wooden door, even a bicycle leaning against the wall.","“That’s our first house,” his father said from the doorway. “In Unaizah. You were four. You used to ride that bicycle round and round the palm.” He paused. “I build towers for strangers. I made this one for me. I wanted to remember the years I was actually there.”","Yasir looked at the little bicycle for a long time. Then he pulled out the chair beside the desk. “Show me how you made the windows,” he said."]},{"title":"The Guest Book","genre":"story","paras":["Murad’s family ran a small guesthouse in the mountains near Al-Baha, and every evening Murad read the guest book aloud to his mother while she cooked. Most entries were kind: thanks for the bread, praise for the view, drawings of the terraced fields by children.","One evening, he stopped reading. A guest had written, in sharp, slanting letters: “The rooms are cold, the water is weak and the owners talk too much. I will not return.”","Murad felt his face burn. His mother had been up since five; his father had carried that guest’s heavy suitcase up three flights of stairs. He reached for a pen to cross the entry out.","His mother caught his wrist. “Leave it,” she said.","“But it’s unfair!”","“Some of it,” she agreed. “But read it again.” He did. “The rooms are cold,” she repeated. “Are they?” Murad thought of the broken heater on the second floor, the one his father kept promising to fix. “The water is weak.” The pump. Also on the list. She stirred the pot. “A guest who is angry tells you what a guest who is polite will not.”","The next week, Murad and his father fixed the heater and replaced the pump. Murad left the harsh entry in the book. Beneath it, months later, a new guest wrote: “Warm rooms, strong water, and owners who talk just enough.”"]},{"title":"Diriyah: Where a State Began","genre":"informational","paras":["On the north-western edge of Riyadh, beside the green line of Wadi Hanifa, stand the mud-brick ruins of At-Turaif, a district of the old town of Diriyah. Its palaces and walls, built in the distinctive Najdi style, may look modest beside the glass towers of the modern capital. Yet this is where the history of the Saudi state began.","**A Capital in the Desert**","In 1727, Imam Muhammad bin Saud became ruler of Diriyah, and the town became the centre of what historians call the first Saudi state. For nearly a century it grew in power and influence. Its builders used materials suited to the climate: thick mud-brick walls kept rooms cool in summer and warm in winter, and narrow streets provided shade.","**Destruction and Memory**","In 1818, after a long siege, Diriyah was captured and largely destroyed. The capital later moved to Riyadh, and At-Turaif was slowly abandoned. But it was never forgotten. In 2010, At-Turaif was added to the UNESCO World Heritage List as an outstanding example of Najdi architecture.","**Restoring the Past**","In recent years, major restoration projects have rebuilt walls and buildings using traditional methods and materials, and museums now tell the district’s story. Since 2022, the Kingdom has marked Founding Day each year on 22 February to commemorate the founding in 1727. Restorers face a difficult balance: making the site welcoming to visitors while keeping it authentic, so that what people see is history rather than imitation."]},{"title":"Small Pieces, Big Problem: Microplastics","genre":"informational","paras":["Plastic does not simply disappear. Over time, sunlight, waves and friction break larger pieces into smaller and smaller fragments. Pieces smaller than five millimetres are called **microplastics**, and scientists now find them almost everywhere they look: in the deep ocean, in Arctic ice, in soil, in drinking water and even in the air.","Some microplastics come from the breakdown of bottles, bags and fishing nets. Others are released directly: tiny fibres shed from synthetic clothing in the washing machine, and particles worn away from car tyres on the road.","Because they are so small, microplastics are eaten by plankton, fish and shellfish, and they can move up the food chain. Researchers have detected them in human blood and tissues. What this means for health is not yet clear. Studies are under way, but scientists caution that the long-term effects are still uncertain.","Solutions are being explored at many levels. Some countries have banned microbeads in cosmetics. Engineers are designing washing-machine filters to catch fibres. But many researchers argue that the most effective step is the simplest: producing and discarding less plastic in the first place."]},{"title":"The Precision of Words","genre":"sentences","paras":["Roots reveal meaning: **path** means *feeling*, so **empathy** is the ability to share another person’s feelings, while **apathy** is a lack of feeling or interest.","Tone depends on word choice. A *thrifty* person and a *miserly* person both spend little money, but the first word praises and the second criticises.","An **allusion** draws on shared stories: a “Pyrrhic victory” refers to a king whose victory cost so many soldiers that it was nearly a defeat.","A **paradox** seems contradictory but reveals a truth: “The more you learn, the more you realise how little you know.”"]},{"title":"The Mock Trial","genre":"drama","paras":["*(A school hall set up as a courtroom for a mock trial. JUDGE (a teacher), PROSECUTOR (HAMAD, 16), DEFENCE (SALEM, 16). The case: a student accused of breaking a science-lab window. The audience already knows, from the opening scene, that the window was broken by a falling branch during a storm.)*","HAMAD: *(confidently)* The defendant was seen near the lab at 4:15. The window was found broken at 4:30. He had motive — he failed his science test that morning. The case is simple.","SALEM: *(standing slowly)* Simple cases are dangerous, Your Honour. They make us stop asking questions. *(He turns to the audience.)* What was the weather like at 4:20?","HAMAD: *(laughing)* Irrelevant.","SALEM: Is it? *(He holds up a photograph.)* This was taken by the caretaker at 4:25. A storm. And this — *(another photograph)* — is the branch lying inside the lab.","*(Silence. HAMAD looks at his notes, then at the photograph.)*","HAMAD: *(quietly, after a pause)* I… withdraw the charge.","JUDGE: *(to the audience)* The verdict, then, is not about this boy. It is about how quickly we decide we already know the answer."]},{"title":"Elegy for the Old Souq","genre":"poem","paras":["They took the roof of palm and beam,","They took the doors of carved teak brown,","And where the spice-sellers would dream","They laid the marble, cold and clean,","And called it progress for the town.","","The new mall glitters, cool and wide;","Its fountains sing a borrowed song.","My father walks its halls with pride,","Then stops beside a pillar’s side","And says, “The cardamom was strong","","Right here — or here — I can’t be sure.”","He laughs, but something in him stays","Beside a vanished wooden door,","Inhaling, through a polished floor,","The scent of other, older days."],"poem":true},{"title":"A Speech to the Graduating Class","genre":"speech","paras":["Graduates, families, teachers: today you are celebrating an ending. I want to talk about beginnings.","When I was your age, I failed my final mathematics exam. Not by a little — by a lot. I remember walking home, holding the result in my pocket as if it could burn through the fabric. I believed that day that my future had closed. It had not. It had simply changed direction.","You will fail too. Not once, but many times. You will fail at tests, at friendships, at plans you thought were perfect. And each time, you will face the same choice: to stop, or to learn. The world does not reward those who never fall. It rewards those who get up, who look honestly at what went wrong, and who try again — wiser.","Some will tell you that success is about talent. Talent matters. But I have met talented people who became complacent, and ordinary people who became extraordinary through effort. Which will you be?","So aspire high. Work hard. Fail well. And when you stand where I am standing, years from now, I hope you will tell a new class the story of how you got up."]},{"title":"Space Tourism Is Not Worth Its Cost","genre":"argument","paras":["In recent years, a handful of wealthy people have paid enormous sums for brief trips to the edge of space. Supporters call it the dawn of a new age of exploration. I disagree. Space tourism, at least in its current form, is an expensive luxury the planet cannot afford.","First, consider the environmental cost. Rocket launches release gases and particles high in the atmosphere, where they may linger far longer than pollution at ground level. Scientists are still studying the effects, but a rapid increase in launches for entertainment could add to atmospheric damage.","Second, consider fairness. The price of a single seat could fund clean-water projects for thousands of people. It is difficult to justify such spending on a few minutes of weightlessness for the very rich.","Supporters argue that space tourism drives innovation and will eventually make space travel cheaper for everyone. This is possible: aviation, too, was once only for the wealthy. But aviation developed alongside real practical needs, such as moving mail and cargo. Space tourism, by contrast, offers little benefit beyond the experience itself.","Exploration is noble. Joyrides are not. We should invest in space science that serves humanity, not in brief adventures for the privileged few."]},{"title":"Text 1: The Engineer’s Notebook · Text 2: Water Security in the Gulf","genre":"paired","paras":["**Text 1: The Engineer’s Notebook (a narrative)**","At three in the morning, the alarm on Membrane Line 4 went off again. Engineer Huda Al-Otaibi walked the length of the plant, past the humming pumps, and checked the pressure readings by torchlight. Somewhere beyond the walls, half a million people were asleep, and every one of them would turn on a tap in a few hours without thinking about it. She wrote in her notebook: “Fixed. Again. Nobody will know.” Then she added, after a moment: “That is the point.”","**Text 2: Water Security in the Gulf (an article)**","With few rivers and limited rainfall, Gulf countries depend heavily on desalination for drinking water. Saudi Arabia is among the world’s largest producers of desalinated water. Most modern plants use reverse osmosis, which forces sea water through membranes that block salt. Desalination requires large amounts of energy, and the salty brine it leaves behind must be managed carefully to protect marine life. Because ancient underground aquifers are being used faster than they refill, experts argue that conservation, water recycling and more efficient technology are all essential to long-term water security."]},{"title":"Text 1: The Last Pearl Season · Text 2: The Ghawas and the End of an Industry","genre":"paired","paras":["**Text 1: The Last Pearl Season (a narrative)**","My grandfather made his last dive in the summer the cultured pearls arrived in the markets of Bahrain. He came home with a pouch of small, uneven pearls, the kind that would once have fed us for a month. The merchant offered him a fraction of their worth. “The Japanese grow them now,” the merchant said, “round as moons, cheap as dates.” My grandfather did not argue. He put the pouch in a drawer, where it stayed for forty years, and the next season he found work on a building site in the new oil town. He never spoke of the sea, but every evening he sat facing it.","**Text 2: The Ghawas and the End of an Industry (an article)**","For generations, pearl diving was the backbone of the Gulf economy. Divers, known as *ghawas*, descended repeatedly during the summer season, often with little more than a nose clip and a stone weight. In the 1920s and 1930s, however, cultured pearls produced in Japan began to reach world markets in large numbers, and prices for natural pearls fell sharply. The global economic depression of the 1930s reduced demand further. Within a few decades, the oil industry had replaced pearling as the region’s main source of wealth, and many former divers found new work in construction and the oil fields."]}]};
(function(d){d.units.forEach(function(u){u.sets.forEach(function(s){s.passage=s.p==null?null:d.pass[s.p];});});})(window.MAP_DATA["reading_9"]);
